window.NY_ROOMS = [{"id":"bowery","name":"The Bowery","area":"LOWER MANHATTAN","mood":"After hours","color":"#79d4e9","description":"Cast iron storefronts, brick tenements, fire escapes and a street that never quite sleeps.","signatures":"Cast iron bases with brick uppers, fire escapes, roof tanks, an elevated girder crossing.","daylit":false,"index":1,"place":"The Bowery","fact":"The Bowery is the oldest thoroughfare in Manhattan. It began as a Lenape trail and took its name from the Dutch bouwerij, the farm road that led to Peter Stuyvesant's estate. In the 19th century it held theaters and beer halls, and later became the city's skid row. The club CBGB opened at 315 Bowery in 1973 and launched punk rock in New York.","year":"1973","learn":"Manhattan's oldest road began as a Lenape trail and a Dutch farm lane.","source":{"name":"Encyclopaedia Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Bowery"},"keywords":["the Bowery","Bowery history","CBGB 315 Bowery","Bowery Mission","Bowery skid row","Stuyvesant bouwerij"],"article":"triangle-fire-1911","articles":["triangle-fire-1911","blackout-1977","the-bodega"]},{"id":"subway","name":"Below the city","area":"THE SUBWAY","mood":"Last train","color":"#e2ca89","description":"White tile vaults, riveted columns, a silver train at the platform and the yellow edge.","signatures":"Glazed tile vaults, mosaic name plaques, riveted I columns, the tactile platform edge.","daylit":false,"index":2,"place":"The New York City Subway","fact":"The first subway line opened on October 27, 1904, run by the Interborough Rapid Transit Company. It ran from City Hall north to 145th Street. The architects Heins and LaFarge designed the original stations with ceramic tile and mosaic name plaques. The system now runs 24 hours a day on more than 470 stations.","year":"1904","learn":"The first train left City Hall on October 27, 1904, and the system never closed.","source":{"name":"Metropolitan Transportation Authority","url":"https://new.mta.info"},"keywords":["New York City subway","IRT 1904","subway history","City Hall station","subway tile","MTA subway map"],"article":"the-subway-1904","articles":["the-subway-1904"]},{"id":"met","name":"Above the park","area":"THE MET ROOF GARDEN","mood":"Golden hour","color":"#dabb96","description":"A limestone terrace over the treetops, sculpture commissions in enamel, the skyline turning gold.","signatures":"Limestone parapets over the park canopy, freestanding sculpture on the terrace, the midtown skyline as backdrop.","daylit":true,"index":3,"place":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Iris and B. Gerald Cantor Roof Garden","fact":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art was founded in 1870 and opened its Fifth Avenue building in 1880, designed by Calvert Vaux and Jacob Wrey Mould. The Iris and B. Gerald Cantor Roof Garden opened in 1987 above the modern wing. Since then the museum has invited one artist each season to make a work for the roof.","year":"1987","learn":"The Met's roof has hosted one artist's commission each season since 1987.","source":{"name":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Met roof garden","Cantor Roof Garden","Metropolitan Museum of Art","Met rooftop commission","Met museum Central Park view","Met museum history"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"brooklyn","name":"Across the river","area":"BROOKLYN WATERFRONT","mood":"Blue hour","color":"#91bacf","description":"A granite promenade under the bridge, weathered steel screens on the river side, Manhattan lit across the water.","signatures":"Gothic pointed arches in the towers, sagging main cables with vertical suspenders and diagonal stays, the deck truss.","daylit":true,"index":4,"place":"Brooklyn Bridge Park","fact":"Brooklyn Bridge Park covers 85 acres of former shipping piers along the East River in Brooklyn Heights and Dumbo. Michael Van Valkenburgh Associates designed it. The first section, Pier 1, opened in 2010, and Jane's Carousel opened in its glass pavilion the next year. Granite from the old Roosevelt Island bridge went into the park's steps and seating.","year":"2010","learn":"The park under the bridge was working shipping piers until 2010.","source":{"name":"Brooklyn Bridge Park","url":"https://www.brooklynbridgepark.org"},"keywords":["Brooklyn Bridge Park","Dumbo waterfront","Jane's Carousel","Pier 1 Brooklyn","Brooklyn Heights Promenade","Empire Fulton Ferry"],"article":"brooklyn-bridge-1883","articles":["brooklyn-bridge-1883"]},{"id":"times","name":"Electric crossroads","area":"TIMES SQUARE","mood":"Midnight","color":"#e477bc","description":"A canyon of lit billboards showing nothing but New Yorkers, the red steps and the ball at the end.","signatures":"Stacked wraparound screens, the red TKTS staircase, One Times Square with its mast and ball.","daylit":false,"index":5,"place":"Times Square","fact":"The crossroads at Broadway and Seventh Avenue was called Longacre Square until 1904, when The New York Times moved into its tower there and the city renamed it. The first New Year's Eve ball dropped from that tower on December 31, 1907. The red glass steps above the TKTS booth opened in 2008.","year":"1904","learn":"It was Longacre Square until a newspaper moved in and the city renamed it in 1904.","source":{"name":"Times Square Alliance","url":"https://www.timessquarenyc.org"},"keywords":["Times Square","Times Square history","New Year's Eve ball drop","TKTS red steps","Longacre Square","Times Square billboards"],"article":"blackout-1977","articles":["blackout-1977","times-square-ball-drop"]},{"id":"ferry","name":"The harbor crossing","area":"STATEN ISLAND FERRY","mood":"Sunset crossing","color":"#f8af72","description":"Orange decks, white rails, the cabin walls hung with New Yorkers, Liberty off the port side.","signatures":"The orange hull and white superstructure, side promenade decks, wheelhouse and funnel, Liberty and the skyline off the water.","daylit":true,"index":6,"place":"The Staten Island Ferry","fact":"Ferries have crossed between Staten Island and Manhattan since the early 1800s. The City of New York took over the service in 1905. The fare was dropped in 1997, and the ride has been free since. The boats carry cars no longer, only people, on a 25 minute crossing past the Statue of Liberty.","year":"1905","learn":"The city has run the boats since 1905, and the ride has been free since 1997.","source":{"name":"NYC Department of Transportation","url":"https://www.nyc.gov/html/dot/html/ferrybus/staten-island-ferry.shtml"},"keywords":["Staten Island Ferry","free ferry New York","Staten Island Ferry schedule","Whitehall Terminal","St. George Terminal","ferry Statue of Liberty view"],"article":"liberty-and-ellis-island","articles":["liberty-and-ellis-island","staten-island-ferry"]},{"id":"botanical","name":"The living city","area":"NEW YORK BOTANICAL GARDEN","mood":"Morning light","color":"#a4c7a1","description":"A glass conservatory nave under a great dome, palms and lily water, the census set among the planting.","signatures":"The glazed nave and central dome with lantern, white iron ribs, tropical planting and a still pool.","daylit":true,"index":7,"place":"The New York Botanical Garden, Enid A. Haupt Conservatory","fact":"The New York Botanical Garden was founded in 1891 on 250 acres in the Bronx. Its glasshouse, now the Enid A. Haupt Conservatory, opened in 1902. The firm Lord and Burnham designed it after the Palm House at Kew. The garden also holds a 50 acre stand of old growth forest that was never cleared.","year":"1902","learn":"The glass conservatory opened in 1902, modeled on the Palm House at Kew.","source":{"name":"New York Botanical Garden","url":"https://www.nybg.org"},"keywords":["New York Botanical Garden","Enid A. Haupt Conservatory","NYBG Bronx","NYBG orchid show","Thain Family Forest","Lord and Burnham glasshouse"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"penn","name":"Arrivals / departures","area":"PENN / MOYNIHAN TRAIN HALL","mood":"The morning rush","color":"#bddce7","description":"Three steel trusses under a skylight, a hanging clock, departure boards that list New Yorkers instead of trains.","signatures":"Exposed riveted trusses, the vaulted skylight lattice, the suspended Art Deco clock, limestone walls.","daylit":true,"index":8,"place":"Moynihan Train Hall","fact":"Moynihan Train Hall opened on January 1, 2021, inside the James A. Farley Post Office. McKim, Mead and White designed the post office, which opened in 1914 across Eighth Avenue from the original Pennsylvania Station. That station was demolished in 1963, and its loss helped start the city's landmarks law. Skidmore, Owings and Merrill designed the new hall under a skylight held by the old mail sorting trusses.","year":"2021","learn":"The trusses over the hall once held up a mail sorting room.","source":{"name":"Moynihan Train Hall","url":"https://moynihantrainhall.nyc"},"keywords":["Moynihan Train Hall","Penn Station","Farley Post Office","original Penn Station demolition","Moynihan Train Hall clock","Amtrak New York"],"article":"the-subway-1904","articles":["the-subway-1904","grand-central-1913"]},{"id":"grand","name":"Under the stars","area":"GRAND CENTRAL TERMINAL","mood":"Timeless","color":"#b6c5ad","description":"The concourse under a celestial vault, light falling through the great arched windows, the brass clock at the centre.","signatures":"The green celestial ceiling with gold constellations, the arched east and west windows, the opal faced clock on the information booth, marble everywhere.","daylit":false,"index":9,"place":"Grand Central Terminal","fact":"Grand Central Terminal opened on February 2, 1913. Reed and Stem and Warren and Wetmore designed it for the New York Central Railroad. The ceiling of the main concourse shows the zodiac, painted backward, after a sketch by Paul Helleu. In 1978 the Supreme Court upheld the terminal's landmark status in Penn Central v. New York City.","year":"1913","learn":"The zodiac on the ceiling is painted backward, and it has been since 1913.","source":{"name":"Grand Central Terminal","url":"https://www.grandcentralterminal.com"},"keywords":["Grand Central Terminal","Grand Central ceiling","Grand Central clock","Grand Central history","Vanderbilt Hall","Penn Central v New York City"],"article":"the-subway-1904","articles":["the-subway-1904","grand-central-1913"]},{"id":"highline","name":"The city, rewilded","area":"THE HIGH LINE","mood":"Late afternoon","color":"#acbf89","description":"An elevated rail bed gone to grass, concrete planks and peel up benches, art on the brick walls that press in on both sides.","signatures":"Tapered concrete planks fingering into planting, the old rails and ties, peel up benches, tight brick and glass facades.","daylit":true,"index":10,"place":"The High Line","fact":"The High Line opened in 1934 as an elevated freight railroad along Manhattan's west side. The last train ran in 1980. Joshua David and Robert Hammond founded Friends of the High Line in 1999 to save it. The first section of the park opened in 2009, designed by James Corner Field Operations, Diller Scofidio and Renfro, and the planting designer Piet Oudolf.","year":"2009","learn":"Freight trains ran here until 1980. The park opened in 2009.","source":{"name":"Friends of the High Line","url":"https://www.thehighline.org"},"keywords":["High Line park","High Line history","High Line freight railroad","Friends of the High Line","Piet Oudolf High Line","High Line art"],"article":"high-line-rewilded-city","articles":["high-line-rewilded-city"]},{"id":"coney","name":"At the edge of it all","area":"CONEY ISLAND","mood":"Summer dusk","color":"#eec092","description":"The boardwalk at dusk, the wheel turning, the Cyclone roaring, the census hung on bathhouse walls facing the sea.","signatures":"The timber boardwalk and white rails, the wheel with swinging cars, the wooden coaster trestles, the Parachute Jump lattice.","daylit":true,"index":11,"place":"Coney Island","fact":"Coney Island became the city's seaside playground in the late 1800s, with Steeplechase Park opening in 1897, Luna Park in 1903, and Dreamland in 1904. The Riegelmann Boardwalk opened in 1923. The Wonder Wheel has turned since 1920 and the Cyclone since 1927, and both are city landmarks.","year":"1920s","learn":"The boardwalk opened in 1923. The Wonder Wheel had already turned for three years.","source":{"name":"NYC Parks, Coney Island Beach and Boardwalk","url":"https://www.nycgovparks.org/parks/coney-island-beach-and-boardwalk"},"keywords":["Coney Island","Coney Island boardwalk","Wonder Wheel","Luna Park Coney Island","Nathan's Famous","Coney Island history"],"article":"coney-island","articles":["coney-island"]},{"id":"bethesda","name":"The painted threshold","area":"BETHESDA ARCADE","mood":"Morning in the park","color":"#c4ad83","description":"Sandstone arches under a patterned tile ceiling, the fountain and the lake beyond the far arch.","signatures":"The encaustic tile ceiling in coffers, the sandstone arcade with carved niches, the fountain terrace and lake beyond.","daylit":true,"index":12,"place":"Bethesda Terrace and Arcade, Central Park","fact":"Calvert Vaux and Jacob Wrey Mould designed Bethesda Terrace as the formal heart of Central Park, built in the 1860s. The arcade ceiling holds about 16,000 Minton encaustic tiles, the only such tile ceiling in the world. Emma Stebbins's fountain, Angel of the Waters, was dedicated in 1873 to mark the Croton Aqueduct that brought clean water to the city.","year":"1873","learn":"The tile ceiling is the only Minton tile ceiling in the world.","source":{"name":"Central Park Conservancy","url":"https://www.centralparknyc.org"},"keywords":["Bethesda Terrace","Bethesda Fountain","Angel of the Waters","Minton tile ceiling Central Park","Bethesda Arcade","Emma Stebbins"],"article":"central-park-greensward","articles":["central-park-greensward"]},{"id":"oculus","name":"White ribs, infinite city","area":"THE OCULUS","mood":"Pure daylight","color":"#c6e4ef","description":"Tapered white ribs closing over a long skylight, marble underfoot, the census standing on white fins in the light.","signatures":"Paired tapering steel ribs meeting at a central skylight spine, a white marble hall, mezzanine galleries behind glass.","daylit":true,"index":13,"place":"The Oculus, World Trade Center Transportation Hub","fact":"The World Trade Center Transportation Hub opened in March 2016. Santiago Calatrava designed it, and it cost about four billion dollars, the most ever spent on a train station. Its ribs form a skylight 355 feet long that opens each September 11. It connects the PATH trains to the subway and the towers of the rebuilt site.","year":"2016","learn":"The skylight along the spine opens once a year, on September 11.","source":{"name":"Port Authority of New York and New Jersey","url":"https://www.panynj.gov"},"keywords":["the Oculus","World Trade Center Transportation Hub","Calatrava Oculus","Oculus PATH station","Westfield World Trade Center","Oculus skylight September 11"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"guggenheim","name":"Endless ascent","area":"THE GUGGENHEIM SPIRAL","mood":"A continuous journey","color":"#e9debf","description":"One helical ramp rising three turns under a radial skylight, the works following the curve of the outer wall.","signatures":"A continuous helical ramp widening as it rises, banded parapets, the ribbed circular skylight, the open rotunda void.","daylit":true,"index":14,"place":"Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum","fact":"The Guggenheim Museum opened on October 21, 1959, on Fifth Avenue at 89th Street. Frank Lloyd Wright designed it as a single ramp spiraling up a quarter mile under a skylight. Wright died six months before it opened. In 2019 UNESCO added it to the World Heritage List with seven other Wright buildings.","year":"1959","learn":"Wright died six months before his spiral opened in 1959.","source":{"name":"Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum","url":"https://www.guggenheim.org"},"keywords":["Guggenheim Museum","Guggenheim spiral","Frank Lloyd Wright Guggenheim","Guggenheim New York","Guggenheim rotunda","Guggenheim tickets"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"library","name":"City of stories","area":"NYPL ROSE READING ROOM","mood":"Quiet afternoon","color":"#c4a47a","description":"Long oak tables under bronze lamps, book galleries on the walls, and paintings set into the carved ceiling coffers.","signatures":"Rows of reading tables with bronze lamps, two tier bookcase walls with a gallery, tall arched windows, a gilded coffered ceiling with painted panels.","daylit":false,"index":15,"place":"New York Public Library, Rose Main Reading Room","fact":"The library's Fifth Avenue building opened on May 23, 1911, designed by Carrere and Hastings. The main reading room is 78 feet wide, 297 feet long and 52 feet high, with painted skies in the ceiling. It was named for the Rose family in 1998 and reopened in 2016 after a two year restoration. The stone lions at the door were named Patience and Fortitude by Mayor La Guardia.","year":"1911","learn":"The reading room is nearly two blocks long under a painted sky.","source":{"name":"The New York Public Library","url":"https://www.nypl.org"},"keywords":["Rose Main Reading Room","New York Public Library","Schwarzman Building","NYPL 42nd Street","Patience and Fortitude lions","NYPL reading room ceiling"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"apollo","name":"After the applause","area":"THE APOLLO","mood":"From the stage","color":"#dc8c86","description":"A velvet house, gilt boxes and balconies, a lit stage carrying three works the size of a curtain.","signatures":"The proscenium arch with its gilt frame, curved balcony fronts, red velvet seats and drapes, footlights and the marquee lettering.","daylit":false,"index":16,"place":"The Apollo Theater","fact":"The building opened in 1914 as a burlesque house for white audiences. In 1934 it reopened as the Apollo Theater and admitted Black audiences, and Amateur Night began the same year. Ella Fitzgerald won Amateur Night in 1934 at age 17. James Brown, Billie Holiday, and Aretha Franklin all played the stage, and the theater became a National Historic Landmark.","year":"1934","learn":"Ella Fitzgerald won Amateur Night here in 1934 at age 17.","source":{"name":"Apollo Theater","url":"https://www.apollotheater.org"},"keywords":["Apollo Theater","Apollo Theater Harlem","Amateur Night at the Apollo","Apollo Theater history","Ella Fitzgerald Apollo","125th Street Harlem"],"article":"harlem-renaissance-apollo","articles":["harlem-renaissance-apollo"]},{"id":"unisphere","name":"The boroughs in orbit","area":"FLUSHING MEADOWS","mood":"Worlds within worlds","color":"#a5cfdf","description":"The steel globe over its fountain basin, orbit rings, radial panels of New Yorkers around the promenade.","signatures":"The open steel lattice globe with continents in relief, three orbit rings, the circular fountain basin, the pavilion towers beyond.","daylit":true,"index":17,"place":"The Unisphere, Flushing Meadows Corona Park","fact":"The Unisphere was the symbol of the 1964 World's Fair in Flushing Meadows Corona Park, Queens. Gilmore D. Clarke designed the stainless steel globe, 140 feet tall and 120 feet across, and United States Steel built it. The park had also hosted the 1939 World's Fair on what had been an ash dump. The Unisphere became a city landmark in 1995.","year":"1964","learn":"The steel globe stands 140 feet tall on the ground of two World's Fairs.","source":{"name":"NYC Parks, Flushing Meadows Corona Park","url":"https://www.nycgovparks.org/parks/flushing-meadows-corona-park"},"keywords":["Unisphere","Flushing Meadows Corona Park","1964 World's Fair","1939 World's Fair Queens","Unisphere fountain","Queens Museum Unisphere"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"tram","name":"Suspended New York","area":"ROOSEVELT ISLAND TRAM","mood":"Above the East River","color":"#eaa58a","description":"The Manhattan tram station high over Second Avenue, a red cabin docked and open, the bridge and river below.","signatures":"The red glazed cabin under its hanger arm, the cable wheel machinery, the Queensboro Bridge cantilever alongside, the river far below.","daylit":true,"index":18,"place":"The Roosevelt Island Tramway","fact":"The Roosevelt Island Tramway opened in 1976 as the first aerial tramway used for public transit in the United States. It was meant as a stopgap until the subway reached the island, which took until 1989, but riders kept it. The cabins cross the East River beside the Queensboro Bridge in about three minutes. New cabins and towers replaced the originals in 2010.","year":"1976","learn":"Built in 1976 as a stopgap until the subway came. The subway came. The tram stayed.","source":{"name":"Roosevelt Island Operating Corporation","url":"https://rioc.ny.gov"},"keywords":["Roosevelt Island Tram","Roosevelt Island Tramway","tram 59th Street Second Avenue","Roosevelt Island","tram MetroCard OMNY","East River aerial tram"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"cloisters","name":"The quiet city","area":"THE CLOISTERS","mood":"Garden light","color":"#c5b991","description":"Paired stone columns around a planted garth, terracotta roofs, the works hung in the shade of the covered walks.","signatures":"Arcades of paired columns with carved capitals, a quartered herb garden with a central fountain, terracotta tiled lean to roofs.","daylit":true,"index":19,"place":"The Met Cloisters","fact":"The Met Cloisters opened in 1938 in Fort Tryon Park at the north end of Manhattan. John D. Rockefeller Jr. paid for the building and the park, and he bought the New Jersey Palisades across the river to keep the view. Charles Collens designed the museum around pieces of five medieval French cloisters. It holds the Unicorn Tapestries.","year":"1938","learn":"Rockefeller bought the cliffs across the river so the view would never change.","source":{"name":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, The Met Cloisters","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/visit/plan-your-visit/met-cloisters"},"keywords":["The Cloisters","Met Cloisters","Fort Tryon Park","Unicorn Tapestries","Cloisters garden","medieval art museum New York"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"navyyard","name":"The dry dock cathedral","area":"BROOKLYN NAVY YARD","mood":"Industrial dusk","color":"#d4ae78","description":"A stepped granite dry dock, a rusted hull on the blocks, monumental works hung from the gantry over the void.","signatures":"Stepped granite altar walls of the dock, keel blocks and rails on the floor, lattice gantry cranes, the caisson gate to the river.","daylit":true,"index":20,"place":"Brooklyn Navy Yard","fact":"The federal government bought the site on Wallabout Bay in 1801 and built warships there for 165 years. The yard launched the USS Maine, the USS Arizona and the USS Missouri, where Japan surrendered in 1945. About 70,000 people worked there at the peak of the Second World War. The Navy closed it in 1966, and it is now an industrial park with more than 500 businesses.","year":"1801","learn":"The USS Missouri was built here. The war ended on its deck.","source":{"name":"Brooklyn Navy Yard Development Corporation","url":"https://brooklynnavyyard.org"},"keywords":["Brooklyn Navy Yard","Navy Yard dry dock","USS Missouri Brooklyn","Brooklyn Navy Yard history","BLDG 92","Wallabout Bay"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"governors","name":"The harbor observatory","area":"GOVERNORS ISLAND HILLS","mood":"A rising horizon","color":"#a7c496","description":"A winding path climbing the Hills to an overlook, works set into the slope like wind screens, the harbor all around.","signatures":"Sculpted grass hills with a switchback gravel path, granite scramble blocks, the long slide, Liberty and the lower Manhattan skyline across the water.","daylit":true,"index":21,"place":"Governors Island, The Hills","fact":"Governors Island was an Army post from 1794 and a Coast Guard base from 1966 until 1996. In 2003 the federal government sold 150 acres of it to the people of New York for one dollar. The Hills opened in 2016, designed by the Dutch firm West 8 on fill from demolished buildings. Outlook Hill rises 70 feet and looks straight at the Statue of Liberty.","year":"2016","learn":"The island was sold to New York for one dollar in 2003.","source":{"name":"Trust for Governors Island","url":"https://www.govisland.com"},"keywords":["Governors Island","The Hills Governors Island","Governors Island ferry","Outlook Hill","Fort Jay Castle Williams","Governors Island slide"],"article":"staten-island-ferry","articles":["staten-island-ferry"]},{"id":"chrysler","name":"The crown and the lobby","area":"THE CHRYSLER BUILDING","mood":"Deco at dusk","color":"#d9c48a","description":"Lexington Avenue under the steel crown, then the red marble lobby with its painted ceiling and inlaid elevator doors.","signatures":"The terraced steel crown with triangular windows and its needle, eagle gargoyles at the setbacks, the red Moroccan marble lobby with a ceiling mural and lotus elevator doors.","daylit":true,"index":22,"place":"The Chrysler Building","fact":"The Chrysler Building was completed in 1930 to a design by William Van Alen. Its steel spire was assembled in secret inside the tower and raised through the roof to reach 1,046 feet and claim the title of world's tallest building. It held the title for eleven months until the Empire State Building. The lobby ceiling carries a mural by Edward Trumbull, and the elevator doors are inlaid wood.","year":"1930","learn":"The spire went up in secret and made it the tallest building on earth for eleven months.","source":{"name":"Encyclopaedia Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Chrysler-Building"},"keywords":["Chrysler Building","Chrysler Building lobby","Chrysler Building spire","Art Deco New York","William Van Alen","Chrysler Building eagles"],"article":"race-to-the-sky-1930","articles":["race-to-the-sky-1930"]},{"id":"flatiron","name":"The prow","area":"THE FLATIRON","mood":"Noon on the wedge","color":"#c9b79a","description":"The triangular building at the split of two avenues, a plaza of chairs at its point, a wedge shaped gallery inside the prow.","signatures":"The triangular plan with its rounded point, limestone and terracotta tiers of bay windows, the pedestrian plaza with green chairs, the cast iron street clock, the park across the way.","daylit":true,"index":23,"place":"The Flatiron Building","fact":"The Flatiron Building was completed in 1902 on the wedge where Broadway crosses Fifth Avenue at 23rd Street. Daniel Burnham's Chicago firm designed it, and it was first called the Fuller Building. It rises 22 stories on a steel frame and is only about six feet wide at the point. The plaza in front of it was turned over to pedestrians in 2008.","year":"1902","learn":"At its point the building is about six feet wide.","source":{"name":"Encyclopaedia Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Flatiron-Building"},"keywords":["Flatiron Building","Flatiron District","Daniel Burnham Flatiron","Flatiron plaza","Fuller Building 1902","Madison Square Park"],"article":"race-to-the-sky-1930","articles":["race-to-the-sky-1930"]},{"id":"bleachers","name":"Bottom of the ninth","area":"THE BRONX BALLPARK","mood":"Two out, full count","color":"#9fb4d6","description":"You are standing on the warning track in right centre with your back to the wall and the whole bowl in front of you: the skin of the infield raked, four towers of light on the grass, forty thousand people in three decks under the white frieze, and a full count in the bottom of the ninth. The New Yorkers hang along the outfield wall where the advertising would be, and every so often the ball comes out to meet them.","signatures":"The white steel frieze around the top of the upper deck, the navy padded outfield wall with its yellow line and the warning track, the infield skin and the mound, the black batter's eye in centre field, Monument Park behind it, four light towers, the scoreboard over the bleachers, and the bleachers themselves.","daylit":false,"index":24,"place":"Yankee Stadium, the Bronx","fact":"This room stands for Yankee Stadium. The original opened on April 18, 1923, on 161st Street, and Babe Ruth hit its first home run that day. It was the first ballpark in the country to be called a stadium. The team moved across the street to the new Yankee Stadium in 2009, and the old site is now Heritage Field, a public park.","year":"1923","learn":"Babe Ruth hit the first home run here on opening day, 1923.","source":{"name":"New York Yankees","url":"https://www.mlb.com/yankees/ballpark"},"keywords":["Yankee Stadium","Yankee Stadium history","original Yankee Stadium 1923","Bronx bleachers","Monument Park","Yankee Stadium tour"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"deli","name":"Take a number","area":"THE APPETIZING COUNTER","mood":"Sunday morning","color":"#e9c46a","description":"A Lower East Side corner store: neon over the awning, a tin ceiling, glass cases lit from inside, the census in every case and up on the photo wall.","signatures":"The long glass and marble counter, hanging salamis on a rail, a pressed tin ceiling, the numbered ticket dispenser, the corner neon and awning, the photo wall.","daylit":true,"index":25,"place":"The appetizing store: Russ and Daughters and Barney Greengrass","fact":"This room stands for the appetizing store, a New York trade in smoked fish, cured salmon, herring, and cream cheese, the foods eaten with bagels. Kosher law kept fish and dairy apart from the meat of the delicatessen, so the two became separate shops. Joel Russ opened his store on the Lower East Side in 1914 and renamed it Russ and Daughters in 1933, the first business in the country with 'and Daughters' in its name. Barney Greengrass has sold sturgeon on the Upper West Side since 1908.","year":"1914","learn":"Appetizing is fish and dairy. Deli is meat. Kosher law made them two shops.","source":{"name":"Russ and Daughters","url":"https://www.russanddaughters.com"},"keywords":["appetizing store","Russ and Daughters","Barney Greengrass","Lower East Side lox","New York smoked fish","bagel and lox history"],"article":"the-bodega","articles":["the-bodega"]},{"id":"washington","name":"Under the arch","area":"WASHINGTON SQUARE","mood":"Saturday afternoon","color":"#b9c8a4","description":"The marble arch, the fountain plaza, chess tables under the trees, the works set on steel easels around the water.","signatures":"The white marble arch with its relief panels and the avenue framed through it, the round fountain basin with a jet, the chess tables in the southwest corner, the red brick row houses on the north side.","daylit":true,"index":26,"place":"Washington Square Park","fact":"The land was a potter's field from 1797 until 1825, and thousands are still buried under the lawn. It became a parade ground and then a park. Stanford White designed the marble arch, dedicated in 1895 to replace a wooden one built for the centennial of Washington's inauguration. In 1958 neighbors led by Shirley Hayes and Jane Jacobs won the fight to close the park to cars.","year":"1895","learn":"The lawn covers a potter's field. The arch came later, in 1895.","source":{"name":"NYC Parks, Washington Square Park","url":"https://www.nycgovparks.org/parks/washington-square-park"},"keywords":["Washington Square Park","Washington Square Arch","Greenwich Village park","Washington Square fountain","chess tables Washington Square","Stanford White arch"],"article":"triangle-fire-1911","articles":["triangle-fire-1911","stonewall-1969","jane-jacobs-and-robert-moses"]},{"id":"boathouse","name":"The Lullwater","area":"PROSPECT PARK BOATHOUSE","mood":"Late summer","color":"#a9c9b0","description":"A white terracotta boathouse on the lake, its loggia hung with the census, rowboats drifting under the bridge.","signatures":"The Beaux Arts loggia of five arches with a balustraded roof, the terrace over the water, the arched Lullwater bridge, rowboats and swans, the tree line closing the lake.","daylit":true,"index":27,"place":"Prospect Park Boathouse","fact":"The Boathouse on the Lullwater opened in 1905, designed by Helmle, Huberty and Hudswell in white terracotta, after the Library of Sannazaro in Venice. Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux laid out Prospect Park itself in 1867 and called it their best work. The Boathouse was saved from demolition in the 1960s and since 2002 has housed the Audubon Center.","year":"1905","learn":"Olmsted and Vaux called Prospect Park their best work, better than Central Park.","source":{"name":"Prospect Park Alliance","url":"https://www.prospectpark.org"},"keywords":["Prospect Park Boathouse","Lullwater Prospect Park","Prospect Park Audubon Center","Olmsted Vaux Prospect Park","Prospect Park Lake","Boathouse wedding Brooklyn"],"article":"central-park-greensward","articles":["central-park-greensward"]},{"id":"halloffame","name":"The colonnade","area":"HALL OF FAME FOR GREAT AMERICANS","mood":"Bronze afternoon","color":"#c9b08a","description":"A semicircular colonnade on a Bronx bluff, the census where the bronze busts stand, a domed library at its heart.","signatures":"The open air Corinthian colonnade curving around the library, bust pedestals between the columns, the terraced site over the Harlem River, the green copper dome and portico.","daylit":true,"index":28,"place":"The Hall of Fame for Great Americans","fact":"The Hall of Fame for Great Americans is a 630 foot stone colonnade on a bluff above the Harlem River in the Bronx. Stanford White designed it, and it opened in 1901 on the University Heights campus of New York University, wrapped around his Gould Memorial Library. It holds 98 bronze busts of Americans chosen by vote. The campus became Bronx Community College in 1973.","year":"1901","learn":"The first hall of fame in the country is a colonnade of 98 bronze heads in the Bronx.","source":{"name":"Bronx Community College","url":"https://www.bcc.cuny.edu"},"keywords":["Hall of Fame for Great Americans","Bronx Community College Hall of Fame","Gould Memorial Library","Stanford White Bronx","University Heights campus","bronze busts Hall of Fame"],"article":"jane-jacobs-and-robert-moses","articles":["jane-jacobs-and-robert-moses"]},{"id":"twa","name":"Departures, 1962","area":"TWA FLIGHT CENTER","mood":"Jet age","color":"#e07a7a","description":"The swooping concrete terminal at the airport, a sunken red lounge, the flight tubes, a plane on the apron.","signatures":"The winged concrete shell and its four vaults, the sunken conversation pit in red, the split flap board, the curved tube corridors, the airliner at the gate.","daylit":true,"index":29,"place":"The TWA Flight Center, JFK Airport","fact":"Eero Saarinen designed the TWA Flight Center at Idlewild Airport, now JFK. It opened in 1962, a year after his death, with a concrete roof shaped like wings and red carpeted tubes leading to the gates. Trans World Airlines used it until the airline ended in 2001. It reopened in 2019 as the lobby of the TWA Hotel, with a 1958 Lockheed Constellation parked outside as a bar.","year":"1962","learn":"Saarinen's terminal opened in 1962, a year after he died.","source":{"name":"TWA Hotel","url":"https://www.twahotel.com"},"keywords":["TWA Flight Center","TWA Hotel JFK","Eero Saarinen terminal","TWA terminal 1962","Connie cocktail lounge","JFK Terminal 5 history"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"snug","name":"The sailors' rest","area":"SNUG HARBOR","mood":"Staten Island stillness","color":"#c9c3a4","description":"Five Greek temples in a row on a Staten Island lawn, a hall of the census inside the main one, a walled scholar's garden behind.","signatures":"The row of Greek Revival temple fronts with Ionic porticos, the long front lawn, the Music Hall, the Chinese Scholar's Garden with its pond, pavilion and moon gate.","daylit":true,"index":30,"place":"Sailors' Snug Harbor","fact":"Sailors' Snug Harbor opened in 1833 on the north shore of Staten Island as a home for aged and worn out seamen, funded by the will of Robert Richard Randall. Its row of five Greek Revival temples faces the harbor; Minard Lafever designed the central building. They were among the first buildings protected under the city's landmarks law in 1965. The site is now a cultural center with the New York Chinese Scholar's Garden, which opened in 1999.","year":"1833","learn":"A home for old sailors, opened 1833, with five Greek temples facing the harbor.","source":{"name":"Snug Harbor Cultural Center and Botanical Garden","url":"https://snug-harbor.org"},"keywords":["Snug Harbor","Sailors' Snug Harbor Staten Island","Chinese Scholar's Garden Staten Island","Snug Harbor Cultural Center","Staten Island Museum Snug Harbor","Greek Revival Staten Island"],"article":"staten-island-ferry","articles":["staten-island-ferry"]},{"id":"wollman","name":"Ice under the towers","area":"WOLLMAN RINK","mood":"December night","color":"#bcd7ea","description":"The rink in the southeast corner of the park, the works hung on the boards, skaters as light, snow falling on the skyline.","signatures":"The oval rink below the outcrops, the Midtown towers rising over bare trees, the boards, the rental house and terrace, snow on every ledge.","daylit":true,"index":31,"place":"Wollman Rink, Central Park","fact":"Wollman Rink opened in 1950 in the southeast corner of Central Park, paid for by a gift from Kate Wollman in memory of her family. It replaced skating on the Pond, which had drawn crowds since the park opened. The rink sits below the towers of Central Park South and has appeared in many films.","year":"1950","learn":"New Yorkers skated on the Pond here for ninety years before the rink opened in 1950.","source":{"name":"Central Park Conservancy","url":"https://www.centralparknyc.org"},"keywords":["Wollman Rink","Central Park ice skating","Wollman Rink tickets","Central Park skating rink","Wollman Rink history","Central Park South skyline"],"article":"central-park-greensward","articles":["central-park-greensward"]},{"id":"doyers","name":"The bend","area":"DOYERS STREET","mood":"Lantern hour","color":"#e05a4a","description":"The crooked lane in Chinatown under strings of lanterns, a dim sum hall up one flight, the census on every wall.","signatures":"The sharp bend of the lane, tenements with fire escapes, red lanterns strung overhead, the painted mural on the bend, the upstairs banquet hall with round tables and a lazy susan.","daylit":true,"index":32,"place":"Doyers Street, Chinatown","fact":"Doyers Street is a 200 foot lane in Chinatown with a sharp bend in the middle. It takes its name from Hendrik Doyer, who ran a distillery there in the 1700s. In the early 1900s the bend was known as the Bloody Angle for its tong wars. Nom Wah Tea Parlor opened on the street in 1920 and is the oldest dim sum restaurant in the city.","year":"1920","learn":"The bend was called the Bloody Angle. Nom Wah has served dim sum on it since 1920.","source":{"name":"Museum of Chinese in America","url":"https://www.mocanyc.org"},"keywords":["Doyers Street","Chinatown Doyers Street","Nom Wah Tea Parlor","Bloody Angle Chinatown","Chinatown history New York","Doyers Street mural"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"strivers","name":"The parlour floor","area":"STRIVERS' ROW","mood":"Sunday in Harlem","color":"#c98a6a","description":"A block of Harlem row houses, and inside one of them the parlour floor: pocket doors, a piano, the census in gilt on the walls.","signatures":"The unbroken row of brownstones and buff brick with high stoops, the tree lined block, the parlour with its bay window, marble mantel, pocket doors and pressed tin ceiling.","daylit":true,"index":33,"place":"Strivers' Row, the St. Nicholas Historic District, Harlem","fact":"The developer David H. King Jr. built these four rows of houses on West 138th and 139th Streets from 1891 to 1893. Three firms designed them: James Brown Lord, Bruce Price with Clarence Luce, and McKim, Mead and White. They were built for white buyers and stood half empty until they were sold to Black New Yorkers around 1919. The doctors, musicians and lawyers who moved in gave the block its name. It became the St. Nicholas Historic District in 1967.","year":"1891","learn":"Built in 1891 for buyers who never came. Harlem's strivers filled it after 1919.","source":{"name":"NYC Landmarks Preservation Commission","url":"https://www.nyc.gov/site/lpc/index.page"},"keywords":["Strivers' Row","St. Nicholas Historic District","Harlem row houses","King Model Houses","West 138th Street Harlem","Harlem brownstones history"],"article":"harlem-renaissance-apollo","articles":["harlem-renaissance-apollo"]},{"id":"rock","name":"Seventy floors up","area":"TOP OF THE ROCK","mood":"Blue hour","color":"#8fb3d9","description":"The observation decks of a limestone tower, glass panels at the edge, the whole island laid out around the works.","signatures":"The stepped limestone crown of the slab, three terraced decks, safety glass panels between stone piers, the park to the north and the spire to the south, the city as the wall.","daylit":true,"index":34,"place":"Top of the Rock, 30 Rockefeller Plaza","fact":"30 Rockefeller Plaza, first the RCA Building, was completed in 1933 as the centerpiece of Rockefeller Center. Raymond Hood led the design. Its observation deck on the 70th floor opened the same year, styled after the deck of an ocean liner. It closed in 1986 and reopened in 2005. The deck faces Central Park to the north and the Empire State Building to the south.","year":"1933","learn":"The deck was styled after an ocean liner in 1933, then closed for nineteen years.","source":{"name":"Top of the Rock","url":"https://www.topoftherocknyc.com"},"keywords":["Top of the Rock","30 Rockefeller Plaza","Rockefeller Center observation deck","Top of the Rock tickets","RCA Building 1933","Top of the Rock sunset"],"article":"race-to-the-sky-1930","articles":["race-to-the-sky-1930"]},{"id":"frick","name":"The garden court","area":"THE FRICK","mood":"Gilded quiet","color":"#d9cfb0","description":"A Fifth Avenue mansion, the skylit garden court with its fountain and ionic columns, the census where the old masters hang.","signatures":"The limestone mansion behind its lawn and iron fence on Fifth Avenue, the glass roofed garden court with paired ionic columns and a central fountain, the paneled galleries opening off it.","daylit":true,"index":35,"place":"The Frick Collection","fact":"Henry Clay Frick built his mansion on Fifth Avenue at 70th Street in 1913 and 1914, designed by Carrere and Hastings. His will left the house and its paintings to the public. John Russell Pope turned it into a museum, and the Garden Court with its fountain was built over the old carriage court. The Frick Collection opened in 1935 and reopened in 2025 after a renovation that opened the family's upstairs rooms.","year":"1935","learn":"The Garden Court was the carriage court until the house became a museum in 1935.","source":{"name":"The Frick Collection","url":"https://www.frick.org"},"keywords":["Frick Collection","Frick museum","Frick Garden Court","Frick mansion Fifth Avenue","Henry Clay Frick house","Frick Vermeer"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"seaport","name":"The tall ship","area":"SOUTH STREET SEAPORT","mood":"Morning fog lifting","color":"#9fb8c4","description":"Cobblestones, the brick counting houses, a square rigger at the pier, the census hung in the rigging and along the row.","signatures":"The Schermerhorn Row brick warehouses, Belgian block paving, the iron and timber pier, a three masted ship with yards and rigging, the bridge over the rooftops.","daylit":true,"index":36,"place":"South Street Seaport","fact":"South Street was the city's main port in the age of sail. Schermerhorn Row, the brick counting houses on Fulton Street, was built in 1811 and 1812. The Fulton Fish Market traded here from 1822 until it moved to the Bronx in 2005. The South Street Seaport Museum was founded in 1967 and keeps the 1885 ship Wavertree at Pier 16.","year":"1811","learn":"The fish market ran on this cobbled street for 183 years.","source":{"name":"South Street Seaport Museum","url":"https://www.southstreetseaportmuseum.org"},"keywords":["South Street Seaport","Seaport Museum","Schermerhorn Row","Wavertree ship","Fulton Fish Market","Pier 17"],"article":"brooklyn-bridge-1883","articles":["brooklyn-bridge-1883","before-the-city-lenape-new-amsterdam"]},{"id":"vessel","name":"The climb","area":"HUDSON YARDS","mood":"Copper noon","color":"#c98a5a","description":"A vase of interlocking staircases in copper steel, climbed one landing at a time, the works on every parapet, the towers all around.","signatures":"The widening honeycomb of landings and flights, the copper coloured cladding, the plaza and its glass towers, the river and the rail yards below.","daylit":true,"index":37,"place":"Vessel, Hudson Yards","fact":"Vessel opened in March 2019 as the centerpiece of Hudson Yards, built over the West Side rail yards. Thomas Heatherwick designed it: 154 flights of stairs, 2,500 steps and 80 landings in a copper colored steel frame 150 feet tall. It closed in 2021 after a series of deaths and reopened in 2024 with mesh barriers.","year":"2019","learn":"154 flights of stairs that lead nowhere, opened over the rail yards in 2019.","source":{"name":"Hudson Yards","url":"https://www.hudsonyardsnewyork.com"},"keywords":["Vessel Hudson Yards","Hudson Yards","Heatherwick Vessel","Vessel tickets","Vessel reopening","Hudson Yards staircase"],"article":"high-line-rewilded-city","articles":["high-line-rewilded-city"]},{"id":"littleisland","name":"The tulips","area":"LITTLE ISLAND","mood":"Golden hour on the river","color":"#a8c4a0","description":"A park held over the Hudson on concrete tulips, rolling lawns and an amphitheatre facing the water, the works set into the slopes.","signatures":"The field of pot shaped concrete piles of varying height, the undulating planted topography, the amphitheatre bowl toward the river, the bridges from the esplanade.","daylit":true,"index":38,"place":"Little Island","fact":"Little Island opened in May 2021 on the Hudson River at 13th Street, on the site of the old Pier 54, where survivors of the Titanic came ashore. Heatherwick Studio designed the park and MNLA the landscape, on 132 concrete tulips rising from the water. Barry Diller and Diane von Furstenberg paid for it. The amphitheater seats 687 and faces the river.","year":"2021","learn":"Titanic survivors landed at this pier. Now it holds a park on 132 concrete tulips.","source":{"name":"Little Island","url":"https://littleisland.org"},"keywords":["Little Island NYC","Little Island park","Pier 55","Little Island amphitheater","Hudson River Park","Little Island tulips"],"article":"high-line-rewilded-city","articles":["high-line-rewilded-city"]},{"id":"cathedral","name":"The unfinished nave","area":"ST. JOHN THE DIVINE","mood":"Candlelight","color":"#b8a8c8","description":"Amsterdam Avenue, the great west front, then a nave the length of two blocks under a rose window, chapels in the apse, candles everywhere.","signatures":"The unfinished gothic west front with its portal and rose window, a nave of pointed arcades and clerestory, the ribbed vault, the seven chapels of the apse, the great organ.","daylit":true,"index":39,"place":"The Cathedral of St. John the Divine","fact":"The cornerstone was laid in 1892 on Amsterdam Avenue at 112th Street. Heins and LaFarge began it in the Romanesque style, and Ralph Adams Cram recast it as Gothic after 1911. The nave opened in 1941, the day before Pearl Harbor. At 601 feet long it is one of the largest churches in the world, and it is still unfinished.","year":"1892","learn":"Begun in 1892, still unfinished, and one of the largest churches on earth.","source":{"name":"The Cathedral of St. John the Divine","url":"https://www.stjohndivine.org"},"keywords":["St. John the Divine","Cathedral of St. John the Divine","Morningside Heights cathedral","largest cathedral New York","Blessing of the Animals","St. John the Divine rose window"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"queensboro","name":"The elevated","area":"QUEENSBORO PLAZA","mood":"Rush hour","color":"#a0a8d8","description":"An elevated platform over the plaza, trains rolling through on two levels, Manhattan across the river, the census on the platform walls.","signatures":"The double deck steel viaduct, curving tracks and platform canopies, the bridge and the skyline to the west, the glass towers of Long Island City, the traffic under the el.","daylit":true,"index":40,"place":"Queensboro Plaza","fact":"Queensboro Plaza is a two level elevated station in Long Island City at the Queens end of the Queensboro Bridge. It was built under the Dual Contracts of the 1910s, when the city's two subway companies shared the tracks here. The bridge itself opened in 1909, designed by Gustav Lindenthal with Henry Hornbostel. The N, W and 7 trains meet on the platforms with Midtown across the river.","year":"1909","learn":"Two subway companies once shared these elevated tracks. Now three lines meet here.","source":{"name":"Metropolitan Transportation Authority","url":"https://new.mta.info"},"keywords":["Queensboro Plaza station","Queensboro Bridge","Long Island City subway","7 train Queensboro Plaza","Dual Contracts subway","Queensboro Bridge history"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"bushwick","name":"The roof","area":"A BUSHWICK ROOFTOP","mood":"The party at dusk","color":"#e8a04a","description":"A tar roof above the murals, water towers and string lights, the neighbouring walls painted the size of buildings with the census.","signatures":"The tar paper roof with bulkhead and water towers, the painted party walls of the surrounding lofts, string lights on posts, the el and the skyline at dusk, a projector on a bedsheet.","daylit":true,"index":41,"place":"A Bushwick rooftop, above the Bushwick Collective","fact":"This room stands for the rooftops of Bushwick, Brooklyn. The Dutch chartered the town as Boswijck in 1661, and in the 1800s it became the city's brewery district. Since 2011 Joseph Ficalora has invited artists to paint the walls around Troutman Street and St. Nicholas Avenue as the Bushwick Collective, an open air mural gallery that changes every year.","year":"2011","learn":"The walls below have been repainted every year since 2011.","source":{"name":"Brooklyn Public Library, Center for Brooklyn History","url":"https://www.bklynlibrary.org/center-for-brooklyn-history"},"keywords":["Bushwick Collective","Bushwick murals","Bushwick street art","Bushwick rooftop","Bushwick Brooklyn history","Troutman Street murals"],"article":"blackout-1977","articles":["blackout-1977","the-bodega"]},{"id":"cyclone","name":"The ride","area":"THE CYCLONE","mood":"Boardwalk summer","color":"#f2e6c8","description":"The wooden coaster at Coney Island, the station, the lift hill, the first drop, and the works along the queue and the boardwalk. Press W to take the ride.","signatures":"The white timber lattice, the lift hill and the plunging first drop, the low station under its roof, the boardwalk, the beach and the tall parachute tower beyond.","daylit":true,"index":42,"place":"The Coney Island Cyclone","fact":"The Cyclone opened on June 26, 1927, at Surf Avenue and West 10th Street. Vernon Keenan designed it and Harry C. Baker built it, a wooden coaster with an 85 foot first drop. The city saved it from demolition in the 1970s and made it a landmark in 1988. It is on the National Register of Historic Places and still runs each summer.","year":"1927","learn":"A wooden coaster from 1927 with an 85 foot first drop, and still running.","source":{"name":"Luna Park in Coney Island","url":"https://lunaparknyc.com"},"keywords":["Coney Island Cyclone","Cyclone roller coaster","wooden roller coaster Coney Island","Cyclone 1927","Luna Park Coney Island","Cyclone first drop"],"article":"coney-island","articles":["coney-island"]},{"id":"brooklynbridge","name":"Between the cables","area":"THE BROOKLYN BRIDGE","mood":"Golden hour crossing","color":"#c9b08a","description":"The plank promenade above the traffic, the gothic towers, the web of cables, Manhattan ahead and the harbor below, the works hung on the tower piers and the lamp banners.","signatures":"The two granite towers with pointed double arches, four main cables and the diagonal stay web, the raised timber walkway between the roadways, the iron lamps, the river far below.","daylit":true,"index":43,"place":"The Brooklyn Bridge","fact":"The Brooklyn Bridge opened on May 24, 1883. John A. Roebling designed it and died of an injury at the site before work began. His son Washington Roebling took over, and when caisson disease left him bedridden, his wife Emily Roebling carried his instructions to the site for a decade. Its 1,595 foot main span was the longest in the world, and it was the first suspension bridge hung on steel wire.","year":"1883","learn":"Emily Roebling ran the site for a decade while her husband watched from a window.","source":{"name":"Encyclopaedia Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Brooklyn-Bridge"},"keywords":["Brooklyn Bridge","Brooklyn Bridge walk","Brooklyn Bridge history","Roebling Brooklyn Bridge","Emily Roebling","Brooklyn Bridge pedestrian path"],"article":"brooklyn-bridge-1883","articles":["brooklyn-bridge-1883"]},{"id":"panorama","name":"The city on a table","area":"THE QUEENS MUSEUM","mood":"Model dusk cycle","color":"#8ab0c8","description":"The Panorama of the city, every borough in miniature under a hall, its lights cycling from day to dusk, a glass walkway around it, the works on the walls above.","signatures":"The 1964 scale model of all five boroughs with its rivers and bridges, the darkened hall with a raised ramped walkway circling the model, the day to night lighting cycle, the Unisphere outside the glass.","daylit":true,"index":44,"place":"The Panorama of the City of New York, Queens Museum","fact":"The Panorama was built for the 1964 World's Fair at the order of Robert Moses. Raymond Lester and Associates made it: every building in the five boroughs at a scale of one inch to 100 feet, on 9,335 square feet. It was updated in 1992 and holds about 895,000 buildings. The hall around it is the New York City Building from the 1939 fair, where the United Nations General Assembly met from 1946 to 1950.","year":"1964","learn":"Every building in the city in miniature, made for the 1964 fair. The UN met here first.","source":{"name":"Queens Museum","url":"https://queensmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Panorama of the City of New York","Queens Museum","Queens Museum Panorama","1964 World's Fair Queens","New York City Building Flushing Meadows","scale model of New York City"],"article":"consolidation-1898","articles":["consolidation-1898","counting-new-york"]},{"id":"liberty","name":"The climb to the crown","area":"LIBERTY ISLAND","mood":"Harbor morning","color":"#7fb8a8","description":"The eleven pointed star of the old fort, Hunt's pedestal on top of it, and then the spiral inside the copper: up the armature Eiffel built, past the works hung on the inner wall, to the twenty five windows of the crown and the harbour through them. Ferries come and go below all morning and the gulls never land.","signatures":"The eleven pointed star of Fort Wood, Richard Morris Hunt's granite pedestal, the green copper robe and the raised right arm with the torch, the seven rayed crown and its twenty five windows, the tablet reading JULY IV MDCCLXXVI held across her body, the broken chains at her feet, Eiffel's iron armature and the helix stair, and the harbour with its ferries and the city beyond.","daylit":true,"index":45,"place":"The Statue of Liberty, Liberty Island","fact":"The Statue of Liberty was dedicated on October 28, 1886, a gift from the people of France. Frederic Auguste Bartholdi sculpted the copper figure, and Gustave Eiffel engineered the iron frame inside. Richard Morris Hunt designed the pedestal, which stands inside the star shaped walls of Fort Wood. The crown has 25 windows and seven rays, and visitors reach it by a spiral stair.","year":"1886","learn":"Eiffel built the iron inside her. The crown has 25 windows.","source":{"name":"National Park Service, Statue of Liberty National Monument","url":"https://www.nps.gov/stli"},"keywords":["Statue of Liberty","Liberty Island","Statue of Liberty crown tickets","Fort Wood","Bartholdi Statue of Liberty","Statue of Liberty pedestal"],"article":"liberty-and-ellis-island","articles":["liberty-and-ellis-island","staten-island-ferry"]},{"id":"oysterbar","name":"Under the tile vaults","area":"GRAND CENTRAL OYSTER BAR","mood":"Lunch rush","color":"#d8b070","description":"Down the ramp under the whispering gallery into the tiled vaults, the long counters, red checked tables, the works between the arches.","signatures":"The Guastavino herringbone tile vaults, the whispering gallery at the ramp crossing, the U shaped lunch counters with stools, the red and white cloths, the neon over the entrance.","daylit":true,"index":46,"place":"Grand Central Oyster Bar","fact":"The Oyster Bar opened in 1913 with the terminal, under vaulted ceilings of tile by Rafael Guastavino. The arches outside its door form the Whispering Gallery, where a murmur into one corner carries to the opposite one. It closed in 1972 and reopened in 1974 under Jerome Brody, who rebuilt it as a seafood house. A fire in 1997 closed it again, and it reopened within a year.","year":"1913","learn":"Whisper into one corner of the arch outside and it lands in the opposite corner.","source":{"name":"Grand Central Oyster Bar","url":"https://www.oysterbarny.com"},"keywords":["Grand Central Oyster Bar","Oyster Bar Grand Central","Guastavino tile Grand Central","Whispering Gallery Grand Central","oyster pan roast","oldest restaurant Grand Central"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"morgan","name":"Three tiers of books","area":"THE MORGAN LIBRARY","mood":"Reading lamp afternoon","color":"#b8864a","description":"Madison Avenue, the marble palazzo, then the East Room: three storeys of walnut bookcases with two walkable balconies, the painted ceiling, the works between the tiers.","signatures":"The McKim palazzo of dry laid marble, the East Room of triple tiered inlaid walnut bookcases behind bronze grilles, the narrow balconies, the tapestry over the fireplace, the lunettes and the painted ceiling.","daylit":true,"index":47,"place":"The Morgan Library and Museum","fact":"Charles McKim designed the library for J. Pierpont Morgan next to his home on Madison Avenue at 36th Street, built from 1902 to 1906. The East Room holds three tiers of walnut bookcases under a painted ceiling. Morgan's son made it a public institution in 1924. Renzo Piano's expansion in 2006 joined the buildings, and the collection holds three Gutenberg Bibles.","year":"1906","learn":"Three tiers of walnut shelves, built for one banker, opened to everyone in 1924.","source":{"name":"The Morgan Library and Museum","url":"https://www.themorgan.org"},"keywords":["Morgan Library","Morgan Library and Museum","J.P. Morgan library East Room","Morgan Library Gutenberg Bible","McKim Morgan Library","Morgan Library tickets"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"radiocity","name":"The great stage","area":"RADIO CITY MUSIC HALL","mood":"House lights down","color":"#c8963a","description":"Sixth Avenue under the marquee, the grand foyer, then the auditorium: the sunrise arches of the proscenium, the great stage, the works as the show.","signatures":"The neon marquee wrapping the corner, the grand foyer with its gold mural and chandeliers, the telescoping plaster arches of the auditorium radiating from the stage, the deep red seats, the gold curtain.","daylit":false,"index":48,"place":"Radio City Music Hall","fact":"Radio City Music Hall opened on December 27, 1932, as part of Rockefeller Center. Edward Durell Stone was its architect and Donald Deskey designed the interiors. Its proscenium arches rise like a sunrise over a stage 144 feet wide, and the house seats about 6,000. The Rockettes have danced here since it opened. The city made it a landmark in 1978 after plans to close it.","year":"1932","learn":"The arches over the stage are a sunrise, and they have been since 1932.","source":{"name":"Radio City Music Hall","url":"https://www.msg.com/radio-city-music-hall"},"keywords":["Radio City Music Hall","Radio City Christmas Spectacular","Rockettes","Radio City Music Hall tour","Art Deco theater New York","Radio City marquee"],"article":"times-square-ball-drop","articles":["times-square-ball-drop"]},{"id":"woolworth","name":"The cathedral of commerce","area":"THE WOOLWORTH BUILDING","mood":"Gothic gold","color":"#d4c48a","description":"Broadway under the terracotta tower, then the cruciform lobby: gold mosaic vaults, marble, the gothic grilles, the works in the arcade bays.","signatures":"The 792 foot terracotta gothic tower with its green copper crown, the cross shaped lobby with a barrel vault of Byzantine glass mosaic, veined marble walls, bronze gothic tracery, the mezzanine murals.","daylit":true,"index":49,"place":"The Woolworth Building","fact":"The Woolworth Building opened in 1913 on Broadway across from City Hall Park. Cass Gilbert designed it in terracotta Gothic, 792 feet tall, the tallest building in the world until 1930. Frank Woolworth paid for it in cash. President Wilson lit its lights from the White House on opening night, and a preacher named it the Cathedral of Commerce. The lobby is vaulted in gold mosaic.","year":"1913","learn":"Woolworth paid cash for the tallest building in the world in 1913.","source":{"name":"Encyclopaedia Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Woolworth-Building"},"keywords":["Woolworth Building","Woolworth Building lobby","Cass Gilbert Woolworth","Cathedral of Commerce","Woolworth Building tour","tallest building 1913"],"article":"race-to-the-sky-1930","articles":["race-to-the-sky-1930"]},{"id":"wallstreet","name":"The canyon","area":"WALL STREET","mood":"Opening bell","color":"#9aa8b8","description":"The narrow canyon at Broad Street, the Doric steps of Federal Hall, the exchange front with its columns and the giant banner, the crowd at the bell, the works in the canyon.","signatures":"The stone canyon of Wall and Broad, the Greek Revival Federal Hall with its steps and statue plinth, the six Corinthian columns and pediment of the exchange with a banner between them, the flags, the crowds, the cobbles.","daylit":true,"index":50,"place":"Wall Street and Federal Hall","fact":"Wall Street runs along the line of the wooden wall that closed the north side of New Amsterdam in the 1650s. Twenty four brokers signed the Buttonwood Agreement under a tree here on May 17, 1792, the start of the New York Stock Exchange. The Exchange's columned front at 11 Wall Street opened in 1903, designed by George B. Post. Across the street at Federal Hall, George Washington took the oath as first president in 1789.","year":"1792","learn":"A wall, a tree, a handshake in 1792, and a stock exchange.","source":{"name":"National Park Service, Federal Hall National Memorial","url":"https://www.nps.gov/feha"},"keywords":["Wall Street","New York Stock Exchange","Federal Hall","Buttonwood Agreement","Wall Street history","Charging Bull"],"article":"before-the-city-lenape-new-amsterdam","articles":["before-the-city-lenape-new-amsterdam"]},{"id":"lincolncenter","name":"The plaza at eight","area":"LINCOLN CENTER","mood":"Curtain in ten minutes","color":"#e8d8a8","description":"The travertine plaza around the fountain, the opera house arches with the works hung where the murals are, the crystal chandeliers rising, the crowd in evening clothes.","signatures":"The travertine plaza with the round fountain at its centre, the five soaring arches of the opera house glowing through glass, the two great murals in the lobby, the starburst chandeliers, the flanking theaters with their colonnades.","daylit":false,"index":51,"place":"Lincoln Center","fact":"Lincoln Center was built on 16 acres of the Upper West Side cleared under urban renewal in the 1950s, the neighborhood of San Juan Hill. President Eisenhower broke ground in 1959. Philharmonic Hall opened in 1962, and the Metropolitan Opera House opened in 1966, designed by Wallace K. Harrison with two murals by Marc Chagall in its lobby. Its chandeliers rise to the ceiling before each performance.","year":"1962","learn":"The chandeliers rise into the ceiling before every performance.","source":{"name":"Lincoln Center for the Performing Arts","url":"https://www.lincolncenter.org"},"keywords":["Lincoln Center","Metropolitan Opera House","Lincoln Center plaza fountain","Chagall murals Met Opera","David Geffen Hall","Lincoln Center history San Juan Hill"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"belvedere","name":"The castle on the rock","area":"BELVEDERE CASTLE","mood":"October afternoon","color":"#9ab088","description":"Vista Rock, the little castle and its terraces over Turtle Pond and the Great Lawn, the towers of the west side beyond, the works on the ramparts.","signatures":"The granite and schist folly with its tower, the two terraces at different heights, the pond below with turtles and the Delacorte stage, the Great Lawn and the twin towered skyline of Central Park West.","daylit":true,"index":52,"place":"Belvedere Castle, Central Park","fact":"Calvert Vaux and Jacob Wrey Mould designed Belvedere Castle as a folly on Vista Rock, the second highest point in Central Park, completed in 1872. It was built to be looked at from the Mall and had no use but the view. The National Weather Service has taken the city's official weather readings here since 1919. The Conservancy restored it in 1983 and again in 2019.","year":"1872","learn":"The castle had no purpose but the view. Since 1919 it has also read the weather.","source":{"name":"Central Park Conservancy","url":"https://www.centralparknyc.org"},"keywords":["Belvedere Castle","Central Park castle","Vista Rock","Turtle Pond","Central Park weather station","Belvedere Castle view"],"article":"central-park-greensward","articles":["central-park-greensward"]},{"id":"highbridge","name":"The aqueduct walk","area":"THE HIGH BRIDGE","mood":"Blue hour","color":"#8898b8","description":"The oldest bridge in the city, an aqueduct on stone arches across the Harlem River, the walk on top from the Bronx to the water tower on the Manhattan bluff.","signatures":"The tall masonry arches on the Bronx bank and the steel arch over the river, the brick paved walkway with iron railings and lamps, the octagonal water tower on the Manhattan cliff, the river and the Deegan below.","daylit":true,"index":53,"place":"The High Bridge","fact":"The High Bridge opened in 1848 to carry the Croton Aqueduct across the Harlem River into Manhattan, and it is the oldest standing bridge in the city. Its stone arches were built after the Roman model. A steel arch replaced the river spans in 1928 to clear shipping. The Highbridge Water Tower rose on the Manhattan side in 1872. Closed to walkers in 1970, the bridge reopened in 2015.","year":"1848","learn":"The city's oldest bridge carried water, not people, and it opened in 1848.","source":{"name":"NYC Parks, Highbridge Park","url":"https://www.nycgovparks.org/parks/highbridge-park"},"keywords":["High Bridge","High Bridge Harlem River","oldest bridge in New York City","Croton Aqueduct","Highbridge Water Tower","High Bridge walk"],"article":"jane-jacobs-and-robert-moses","articles":["jane-jacobs-and-robert-moses"]},{"id":"arthuravenue","name":"The market hall","area":"ARTHUR AVENUE","mood":"Saturday morning","color":"#c89a5a","description":"The Belmont street of bakeries and butchers, then the covered retail market: stalls under one roof, hanging provolone and salami, the works over the counters.","signatures":"The 1940 market hall with its skylit steel roof, the long aisles of stalls, cheeses and sausages hung from rails, crates of produce, cigar rollers by the door, the avenue of awnings outside.","daylit":true,"index":54,"place":"Arthur Avenue Retail Market, Belmont","fact":"Arthur Avenue in the Belmont section of the Bronx has been the center of an Italian community since the early 1900s. The Arthur Avenue Retail Market opened in 1940, built by the city under Mayor La Guardia to bring pushcart vendors indoors under one roof. Butchers, cheese makers and bakers still run stalls there. The street is named for President Chester A. Arthur.","year":"1940","learn":"La Guardia moved the pushcarts indoors in 1940. The stalls are still there.","source":{"name":"Bronx County Historical Society","url":"https://bronxhistoricalsociety.org"},"keywords":["Arthur Avenue","Arthur Avenue Retail Market","Belmont Bronx Little Italy","Arthur Avenue bakery","Bronx Little Italy","Arthur Avenue market stalls"],"article":"the-bodega","articles":["the-bodega"]},{"id":"wavehill","name":"The pergola over the river","area":"WAVE HILL","mood":"Late light on the Palisades","color":"#a8c8a0","description":"The garden estate in Riverdale: the great lawn, the pergola on the terrace edge, the conservatory, and across the Hudson the cliffs of the Palisades catching the last sun.","signatures":"The long timber pergola on the terrace edge, the flower garden with its rustic fence, the glass conservatory, the fieldstone house, the lawn falling to the river and the wall of the Palisades beyond.","daylit":true,"index":55,"place":"Wave Hill","fact":"Wave Hill House was built in 1843 by William Lewis Morris on a bluff above the Hudson in Riverdale, the Bronx. Theodore Roosevelt's family summered there, Mark Twain rented it from 1901 to 1903, and Arturo Toscanini lived there in the 1940s. The financier George W. Perkins assembled the estate and its gardens. His family gave it to the city in 1960, and it opened as a public garden in 1965.","year":"1965","learn":"Mark Twain rented this house. Toscanini lived here. The city got it in 1960.","source":{"name":"Wave Hill","url":"https://www.wavehill.org"},"keywords":["Wave Hill","Wave Hill garden Bronx","Wave Hill pergola","Riverdale Bronx garden","Palisades view Hudson","Mark Twain Wave Hill"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"gantry","name":"The gantries","area":"GANTRY PLAZA","mood":"Neon on the water","color":"#e05a5a","description":"The Long Island City waterfront: the two iron transfer gantries, the piers reaching into the East River, the great neon sign, the whole Midtown skyline across the water at night.","signatures":"The pair of black riveted gantry frames with their lettering, four long piers with benches and lounge chairs, the red neon bottle sign on its frame, the towers of Midtown mirrored in the river.","daylit":false,"index":56,"place":"Gantry Plaza State Park","fact":"Gantry Plaza State Park opened in 1998 on the East River in Long Island City, Queens. Its two iron gantries, built in 1925, once lifted railcars onto barges bound for Manhattan. The Pepsi-Cola sign came from the bottling plant that stood nearby from 1936; it was moved to the park in 2009 and became a city landmark in 2016. The piers face Midtown across the water.","year":"1998","learn":"The gantries lifted railcars onto barges. The Pepsi sign came from the plant next door.","source":{"name":"New York State Parks","url":"https://parks.ny.gov"},"keywords":["Gantry Plaza State Park","Pepsi sign Long Island City","Long Island City waterfront","Hunters Point gantries","Gantry Plaza piers","LIC skyline view"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"rockaway","name":"Surf at the end of the A","area":"ROCKAWAY BEACH","mood":"Sunrise swell","color":"#f0c890","description":"The concrete boardwalk, the lifeguard chairs, the surfers waiting in the swell, the A train rolling in on the trestle behind the dunes, the works on the beach shacks.","signatures":"The long new concrete boardwalk with its curved shade shelters, the numbered lifeguard chairs, the dune grass and fences, the surf break at the jetties, the elevated A train and the low bungalows.","daylit":true,"index":57,"place":"Rockaway Beach and Boardwalk","fact":"Rockaway Beach runs along the Atlantic side of the Rockaway peninsula in Queens, and NYC Parks calls it the largest urban beach in the United States. Hurricane Sandy destroyed the wooden boardwalk in October 2012, and the city rebuilt it in concrete, finishing in 2017. It holds the only beaches in New York City where surfing is allowed.","year":"2017","learn":"The only beaches in New York City where surfing is allowed, at the end of the A.","source":{"name":"NYC Parks","url":"https://www.nycgovparks.org/parks/rockaway-beach-and-boardwalk"},"keywords":["Rockaway Beach","Rockaway boardwalk","surfing in NYC","A train to Rockaway","Rockaway Beach Queens","Rockaway surf beach"],"article":"coney-island","articles":["coney-island"]},{"id":"greenwood","name":"The gothic gate","area":"GREEN-WOOD CEMETERY","mood":"Autumn gold","color":"#c8a050","description":"The brownstone gothic gate with its parrots, then the hills of the old cemetery: monuments, mausoleums, the harbor glimpsed from the highest ground, the works on the vault doors.","signatures":"The 1861 brownstone gatehouse with three spires and the monk parakeet nests, rolling glacial hills of obelisks and angels, granite mausoleums set into slopes, ancient trees in fall color, the harbor view from Battle Hill.","daylit":true,"index":58,"place":"Green-Wood Cemetery","fact":"Green-Wood Cemetery was founded in 1838 on 478 acres of hills in Brooklyn, laid out by David Bates Douglass. Richard Upjohn designed the Gothic Revival main gate at 25th Street in the 1860s, and a colony of monk parakeets now nests in its spires. Battle Hill, the highest point in Brooklyn, saw fighting in the Battle of Brooklyn in 1776, and the cemetery became a National Historic Landmark in 2006.","year":"1838","learn":"Wild parrots nest in the gate of a cemetery founded in 1838.","source":{"name":"Green-Wood Cemetery","url":"https://www.green-wood.com"},"keywords":["Green-Wood Cemetery","Green-Wood gate parrots","Battle Hill Brooklyn","Green-Wood Cemetery tours","who is buried in Green-Wood","Green-Wood Brooklyn"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"domino","name":"The refinery","area":"DOMINO PARK","mood":"Industrial gold","color":"#d88a4a","description":"The brick shell of the sugar refinery on the Williamsburg waterfront, a glass building grown inside it, the syrup tanks and gantry cranes in the park, the bridge and the skyline over the river.","signatures":"The 1880s brick refinery with its round arched windows and the great smokestack, the modern glass vault rising within the shell, the elevated walkway over the park, the salvaged syrup tanks and cranes, the Williamsburg Bridge.","daylit":true,"index":59,"place":"Domino Park and the Domino Sugar Refinery","fact":"The Havemeyer family's sugar refinery on the Williamsburg waterfront was rebuilt in brick after a fire in 1882, and at its peak it refined more sugar than any plant in the world. The refinery closed in 2004 and the city landmarked the building in 2007. Domino Park, designed by James Corner Field Operations, opened on the site in 2018 with the old syrup tanks and gantry cranes kept as relics.","year":"2018","learn":"A sugar refinery that once led the world, kept as a park on the Williamsburg shore.","source":{"name":"Domino Park","url":"https://www.dominopark.com"},"keywords":["Domino Park","Domino Sugar Factory","Domino Sugar Refinery Williamsburg","Domino Park Brooklyn","Williamsburg waterfront park","Domino refinery landmark"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"boatgraveyard","name":"The ships in the marsh","area":"THE ARTHUR KILL","mood":"Fog on the kill","color":"#7a8a80","description":"Staten Island's western shore: rusting hulls of tugs and ferries settled in the marsh grass, a kayak route through them, herons on the wrecks, the works on the wheelhouses.","signatures":"Dozens of scuttled tugboats, barges and a ferry sunk to their decks in the tidal marsh, rust and peeling paint, cordgrass and mud at low tide, the New Jersey refineries and cranes across the water, fog.","daylit":true,"index":60,"place":"The Arthur Kill boat graveyard, Rossville, Staten Island","fact":"The Arthur Kill is the tidal strait between Staten Island and New Jersey. Since the 1930s the marsh off Arthur Kill Road and Rossville Avenue has been a dumping ground for ships: John J. Witte ran the salvage yard and refused the curious public any access, and as many as 400 vessels once lay there. When Witte died in 1980 the yard passed to his son and became the Donjon Marine Company. Between 25 and 40 hulls remain, among them the fireboat Abram S. Hewitt, which helped rescue victims of the General Slocum disaster in 1904.","year":"1930s","learn":"Four hundred ships once rotted in a Staten Island marsh; a few dozen remain.","source":{"name":"Baruch College, NYCdata Hidden Gems","url":"https://www.baruch.cuny.edu/nycdata/hidden_gems/boat_graveyard.html"},"keywords":["Staten Island boat graveyard","Arthur Kill ship graveyard","Witte Marine scrap yard","Rossville Staten Island","Donjon Marine","Abram S. Hewitt fireboat"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"intrepid","name":"The flight deck","area":"THE INTREPID","mood":"Hudson wind","color":"#8a98a8","description":"Pier 86: the aircraft carrier moored on the Hudson, the flight deck lined with aircraft, the island tower, the shuttle pavilion, the works below in the hangar deck.","signatures":"The grey Essex class carrier with its angled flight deck and island superstructure, the jets and helicopters parked along the deck, the space shuttle pavilion aft, the hangar deck open below, the Hudson and New Jersey beyond.","daylit":true,"index":61,"place":"Intrepid Museum, USS Intrepid","fact":"USS Intrepid, an Essex class aircraft carrier, was commissioned in 1943 and survived kamikaze attacks in the Pacific during World War II. It later served in Vietnam and recovered Mercury and Gemini space capsules. The ship opened as a museum at Pier 86 on the Hudson in 1982, and the Space Shuttle Enterprise joined it on the flight deck in 2012.","year":"1943","learn":"A carrier that took kamikaze hits in 1944 and now holds a space shuttle.","source":{"name":"Intrepid Museum","url":"https://www.intrepidmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Intrepid Museum","USS Intrepid","Intrepid Sea Air and Space Museum","Space Shuttle Enterprise New York","Pier 86 aircraft carrier","Intrepid flight deck"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"studio8h","name":"Live from New York","area":"STUDIO 8H","mood":"Eleven thirty Saturday","color":"#e8b040","description":"The lobby of the tower on the plaza, the elevator up, then the studio: the home base stage, the band stand, the cameras, the bleachers, the cue cards, the works on every set wall.","signatures":"The art deco lobby with its dark marble and gold leaf ceiling, the studio floor with its brick home base arch and the musical guest stage, the boom cameras and the applause sign, the steep audience bleachers, the cue card easel.","daylit":false,"index":62,"place":"Studio 8H, 30 Rockefeller Plaza","fact":"Studio 8H was built in 1933 as the largest radio studio in the world, and Arturo Toscanini conducted the NBC Symphony Orchestra there from 1937 to 1954. Saturday Night Live first aired from the studio on October 11, 1975, created by Lorne Michaels, with George Carlin as the first host. The show has broadcast live from the same room ever since.","year":"1975","learn":"Toscanini's radio studio became the home of Saturday Night Live in 1975.","source":{"name":"NBC, Saturday Night Live","url":"https://www.nbc.com/saturday-night-live"},"keywords":["Studio 8H","Saturday Night Live studio","SNL 30 Rock","30 Rockefeller Plaza","NBC Studios tour","Lorne Michaels SNL"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"carnegie","name":"The horseshoe","area":"CARNEGIE HALL","mood":"Concert black","color":"#c8a070","description":"Fifty seventh Street, the Italianate brick front, then the hall: five curved tiers in cream and gold around the stage, the works in the boxes, practice, practice, practice.","signatures":"The brown brick and terracotta facade with its arched windows, the cream and gold horseshoe auditorium with five levels curving to the stage, the red seats, the plain proscenium and the organ grille, the dressing room corridor.","daylit":false,"index":63,"place":"Carnegie Hall","fact":"Carnegie Hall opened in May 1891, paid for by Andrew Carnegie and designed by William Burnet Tuthill, with Tchaikovsky conducting on opening night. Isaac Stern led the campaign that saved it from demolition in 1960, and the city bought the building. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1962, and its main hall now carries Stern's name.","year":"1891","learn":"Tchaikovsky conducted on opening night in 1891.","source":{"name":"Carnegie Hall","url":"https://www.carnegiehall.org"},"keywords":["Carnegie Hall","Carnegie Hall history","Isaac Stern Auditorium","Carnegie Hall tours","Carnegie Hall 57th Street","how do you get to Carnegie Hall"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"vanguard","name":"The wedge downstairs","area":"THE VILLAGE VANGUARD","mood":"Second set","color":"#c83a3a","description":"The red awning on Seventh Avenue South, the narrow stair down, the triangular room: fourteen feet of stage, the piano, the drums, the photographs, the works between them.","signatures":"The red canopy and the steep staircase, the wedge shaped basement with the stage in the point, the red walls hung with photographs of the players, the tiny round tables and bentwood chairs, the kitchen door that never opens.","daylit":false,"index":64,"place":"The Village Vanguard","fact":"Max Gordon opened the Village Vanguard in a basement at 178 Seventh Avenue South in 1935, first for poetry and folk music, then for jazz alone from the late 1950s. Sonny Rollins, John Coltrane and Bill Evans all recorded live albums there. Lorraine Gordon ran the club after Max died in 1989, and the Vanguard Jazz Orchestra has played Monday nights since 1966.","year":"1935","learn":"A basement wedge that has held jazz since 1935.","source":{"name":"The Village Vanguard","url":"https://villagevanguard.com"},"keywords":["Village Vanguard","Village Vanguard jazz club","Village Vanguard schedule","Coltrane Live at the Village Vanguard","oldest jazz club New York","Vanguard Jazz Orchestra"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"rucker","name":"The court","area":"RUCKER PARK","mood":"Summer night game","color":"#f08a2a","description":"One hundred fifty fifth and Frederick Douglass: the fenced court under the lights, the bleachers packed, a game moving, the river and the Bronx beyond, the works on the fence.","signatures":"The full court inside a chain link fence, the aluminium bleachers along both sidelines, the light towers, the Polo Grounds Towers behind, the Harlem River and the Macombs Dam Bridge, a crowd three deep at the fence.","daylit":false,"index":65,"place":"Holcombe Rucker Park","fact":"Holcombe Rucker, a Parks Department worker, started a summer basketball tournament in Harlem in 1950 to keep young players in school. The park at 155th Street and Frederick Douglass Boulevard was later named for him. Julius Erving, Kareem Abdul-Jabbar, Kobe Bryant and Kevin Durant have all played on the court.","year":"1950","learn":"A Parks worker's 1950 tournament made a Harlem court famous.","source":{"name":"NYC Parks","url":"https://www.nycgovparks.org/parks/holcombe-rucker-park"},"keywords":["Rucker Park","Rucker Park Harlem","Holcombe Rucker Park","Rucker Park tournament","streetball Rucker","Entertainer's Basketball Classic"],"article":"birth-of-hip-hop-1973","articles":["birth-of-hip-hop-1973"]},{"id":"sedgwick","name":"The rec room","area":"1520 SEDGWICK AVENUE","mood":"August 1973","color":"#e0c030","description":"The tower over the Cross Bronx, the block party on the plaza, then the rec room downstairs: two turntables and a mixer, the speakers, the break, the works on the cinderblock.","signatures":"The brown brick tower at the top of the hill, the highway trench below, the lobby and the community room with its drop ceiling and cinderblock, two turntables on a folding table, a wall of speakers, a crowd in a circle around the floor.","daylit":false,"index":66,"place":"1520 Sedgwick Avenue, the Bronx","fact":"On August 11, 1973, DJ Kool Herc played a back to school party for his sister Cindy Campbell in the rec room of 1520 Sedgwick Avenue. He isolated and repeated the breaks in records so dancers could keep going, a technique that became the foundation of hip hop. The building beside the Cross Bronx Expressway is widely recognized as the birthplace of the culture.","year":"1973","learn":"A 1973 rec room party in the Bronx started hip hop.","source":{"name":"Britannica, hip-hop","url":"https://www.britannica.com/art/hip-hop"},"keywords":["1520 Sedgwick Avenue","birthplace of hip hop","DJ Kool Herc","Kool Herc party 1973","Sedgwick Avenue Bronx","hip hop history Bronx"],"article":"birth-of-hip-hop-1973","articles":["birth-of-hip-hop-1973"]},{"id":"balloons","name":"The night before the parade","area":"CENTRAL PARK WEST","mood":"Inflation night","color":"#f0a0c0","description":"The blocks beside the museum the night before Thanksgiving: giant balloons under their nets, the crews, the crowd shuffling past under the streetlights, the works on the barricade walls.","signatures":"The Beaux Arts museum front, the side streets closed off, enormous character balloons lying on their sides under cargo nets and sandbags, helium trucks, police barricades, families in coats, bare trees in the park.","daylit":false,"index":67,"place":"Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade balloon inflation, Central Park West","fact":"The Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade began in 1924, and the first giant balloons appeared in 1927. The night before the parade, crews inflate the balloons on the streets around the American Museum of Natural History, and the public walks past them under nets. This room interprets that inflation night, not the parade itself.","year":"1924","learn":"The night before Thanksgiving, the balloons lie under nets beside the museum.","source":{"name":"Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade","url":"https://www.macys.com/s/parade/"},"keywords":["Macy's balloon inflation","Thanksgiving balloon inflation NYC","Macy's parade balloons night before","Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade","Central Park West balloons","balloon inflation 77th Street"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"stonewall","name":"Christopher Street","area":"SHERIDAN SQUARE","mood":"The last Sunday in June","color":"#e83a8a","description":"The little brick bar with its arched windows, the park across the street with the white figures, the banners on every building, the march coming down Christopher with its floats.","signatures":"The two storey brick front with arched windows and the neon in the window, the triangular park with its benches and the four white sculptures, rainbow flags on the fire escapes, floats and marchers filling the narrow street, confetti in the air.","daylit":true,"index":68,"place":"The Stonewall Inn and Christopher Park","fact":"Police raided the Stonewall Inn at 53 Christopher Street on June 28, 1969, and patrons fought back over several nights. The first pride march followed one year later. Christopher Park across the street holds George Segal's Gay Liberation sculpture, and in 2016 the site became Stonewall National Monument, the first national monument to LGBTQ history.","year":"1969","learn":"The 1969 uprising here became the first national monument to LGBTQ history.","source":{"name":"National Park Service, Stonewall National Monument","url":"https://www.nps.gov/ston/"},"keywords":["Stonewall Inn","Stonewall National Monument","Stonewall riots 1969","Christopher Park","Christopher Street Pride","Sheridan Square"],"article":"stonewall-1969","articles":["stonewall-1969"]},{"id":"sangennaro","name":"The feast on Mulberry","area":"THE FEAST OF SAN GENNARO","mood":"Eleven nights in September","color":"#e8503a","description":"Mulberry Street under the arches of lights, the zeppole stands, the sausage smoke, the saint carried down the block, the works between the strings of bulbs.","signatures":"Arches of red white and green lights spanning the street block after block, food stands with striped awnings and steam, the tenements with fire escapes, the statue of the saint on his litter pinned with ribbons, the cannoli.","daylit":false,"index":69,"place":"The Feast of San Gennaro, Mulberry Street","fact":"Immigrants from Naples first held the Feast of San Gennaro on Mulberry Street in 1926 to honor their patron saint, whose feast day falls on September 19. The festival runs eleven days each September along Mulberry Street in Little Italy. A statue of the saint is carried in procession through the street.","year":"1926","learn":"Neapolitan immigrants started the Mulberry Street feast in 1926.","source":{"name":"Feast of San Gennaro","url":"https://www.sangennaronyc.org"},"keywords":["Feast of San Gennaro","San Gennaro festival","Mulberry Street festival","Little Italy feast","San Gennaro dates","San Gennaro NYC"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"chelseahotel","name":"The lobby of the hotel","area":"THE CHELSEA HOTEL","mood":"Room rate paid in paintings","color":"#b8483a","description":"Twenty third Street under the iron balconies, the lobby hung floor to ceiling with the art residents left instead of rent, the stair spiralling up twelve floors, the works in every frame.","signatures":"The red brick front with black iron balconies stacked twelve storeys high, the neon vertical sign, the lobby with its fireplace and salon hang, the famous open stair with iron balusters climbing through the building, the corridors of numbered doors.","daylit":true,"index":70,"place":"The Hotel Chelsea","fact":"The Hotel Chelsea at 222 West 23rd Street opened in 1884 as one of the city's first cooperative apartment buildings, designed by Philip Hubert, and became a hotel in 1905. Mark Twain, Dylan Thomas, Arthur Miller, Bob Dylan, Leonard Cohen and Patti Smith lived there, and Andy Warhol filmed Chelsea Girls in its rooms. The city landmarked it in 1966, and it reopened as a hotel in 2022 after a long renovation.","year":"1884","learn":"Residents paid in paintings, and the lobby still shows them.","source":{"name":"Hotel Chelsea","url":"https://www.hotelchelsea.com"},"keywords":["Hotel Chelsea","Chelsea Hotel history","Chelsea Hotel residents","Chelsea Hotel 23rd Street","Chelsea Hotel Patti Smith","Chelsea Hotel lobby art"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"strand","name":"Eighteen miles of books","area":"THE STRAND","mood":"Rainy Tuesday","color":"#c83a3a","description":"Broadway and Twelfth in the rain, the dollar carts under the awning, then the aisles: tables of new arrivals, the tall stacks, the rare book room upstairs, the works on the end caps.","signatures":"The red awning and the sidewalk carts of one dollar books, the ground floor of long tables and tall wooden stacks under fluorescent light, the tote bags, the staff picks wall, the rolling ladders, the rare book room with its leather and glass.","daylit":true,"index":71,"place":"The Strand Book Store","fact":"Benjamin Bass opened the Strand in 1927 on Fourth Avenue's Book Row, where dozens of bookshops once stood. The store moved to 828 Broadway at 12th Street in 1957 and claims eighteen miles of books. The city landmarked the building in 2019, and the store is still run by the Bass family.","year":"1927","learn":"The last survivor of Fourth Avenue's Book Row, open since 1927.","source":{"name":"Strand Book Store","url":"https://www.strandbooks.com"},"keywords":["Strand Bookstore","Strand Book Store NYC","18 miles of books","Strand Broadway 12th Street","Book Row New York","Strand rare book room"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"dakota","name":"The courtyard and the mosaic","area":"THE DAKOTA","mood":"December dusk","color":"#c8b090","description":"Seventy second and Central Park West: the gabled fortress of an apartment house, the carriage arch into its courtyard, and across the street the round mosaic in the park with its one word, the works on the courtyard walls.","signatures":"The sandstone and brick block with its gables, dormers and iron railings, the deep arched carriage entrance with the sentry box, the square inner courtyard with two fountains, the teardrop park across the avenue with the black and white mosaic ringed with flowers.","daylit":true,"index":72,"place":"The Dakota and Strawberry Fields","fact":"The Dakota at 1 West 72nd Street was completed in 1884 for Edward Clark of the Singer Sewing Machine Company, designed by Henry Janeway Hardenbergh. John Lennon lived there and was shot at its gate on December 8, 1980. Strawberry Fields, the memorial across the street in Central Park with its Imagine mosaic, was dedicated in 1985.","year":"1884","learn":"An 1884 apartment fortress and the mosaic across the street.","source":{"name":"Central Park Conservancy","url":"https://www.centralparknyc.org"},"keywords":["The Dakota","Dakota apartment building","Strawberry Fields Central Park","Imagine mosaic","John Lennon Dakota","Dakota 72nd Street"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"garden","name":"The world's most famous arena","area":"MADISON SQUARE GARDEN","mood":"Fourth quarter, two minutes","color":"#f0842a","description":"Above the train hall, the round drum of the arena: the bowl in blue and orange, the cable ceiling, the scoreboard cube, a game on the floor, the works around the concourse.","signatures":"The circular drum on Seventh Avenue, the bowl of orange and blue seats climbing to the suspended cable roof, the four sided scoreboard over centre court, the hardwood with its lines, the bridges hanging under the ceiling.","daylit":false,"index":73,"place":"Madison Square Garden","fact":"The current Madison Square Garden, the fourth to carry the name, opened in 1968 above Penn Station on the site of the demolished original Pennsylvania Station, designed by Charles Luckman. It is home to the Knicks and the Rangers. Muhammad Ali and Joe Frazier fought there in March 1971, and Willis Reed limped onto its floor for game seven of the 1970 NBA Finals.","year":"1968","learn":"Built over Penn Station in 1968, it has hosted the Knicks ever since.","source":{"name":"Madison Square Garden","url":"https://www.msg.com"},"keywords":["Madison Square Garden","MSG arena","Knicks Madison Square Garden","Madison Square Garden history","Ali Frazier 1971 Garden","Penn Station Madison Square Garden"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"dendur","name":"The temple by the window","area":"THE TEMPLE OF DENDUR","mood":"Snow light through glass","color":"#d8c8a0","description":"The great sloped glass wall on the park, the pool standing in for the Nile, the sandstone gate and temple on their platform, the works along the granite walls of the wing.","signatures":"The Sackler wing with its tilted wall of glass and the park beyond, the reflecting pool with its edge of black granite, the temple and its pylon gateway raised on a stone platform, the sloped granite walls of the hall, the sunlight sliding across the floor.","daylit":true,"index":74,"place":"The Temple of Dendur, The Met","fact":"The Temple of Dendur was built in Nubia around 15 BC under the Roman emperor Augustus and dedicated to Isis and two deified local brothers. Egypt gave it to the United States in 1965 in thanks for help saving monuments from the waters of the Aswan High Dam. The Met's Sackler Wing, designed by Kevin Roche John Dinkeloo and Associates, opened around it in 1978.","year":"15 BC","learn":"A Roman era Egyptian temple given to the United States in 1965.","source":{"name":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Temple of Dendur","Temple of Dendur Met","Sackler Wing Met","Egyptian temple in the Met","Dendur Aswan gift","Met Egyptian art"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"moma","name":"The garden on 54th","area":"THE SCULPTURE GARDEN","mood":"First warm Friday","color":"#8ab0a8","description":"The marble court between the towers: two long pools, the birches and the weeping beech, the bronze figures on their plinths, the glass galleries looking in, the works on the garden walls.","signatures":"The sunken marble court with two rectangular pools crossed by narrow bridges, groves of birches and a weeping beech, bronzes on low plinths, the grey granite wall to the street, the glass curtain walls of the galleries above.","daylit":true,"index":75,"place":"The Abby Aldrich Rockefeller Sculpture Garden, MoMA","fact":"MoMA's first sculpture garden opened in 1939. Philip Johnson redesigned it in 1953 and named it for Abby Aldrich Rockefeller, one of the museum's three founders. Its marble court, pools and trees hold works by Picasso, Rodin, Matisse and Maillol.","year":"1953","learn":"Philip Johnson's 1953 court named for a MoMA founder.","source":{"name":"The Museum of Modern Art","url":"https://www.moma.org"},"keywords":["MoMA sculpture garden","Abby Aldrich Rockefeller Sculpture Garden","Philip Johnson sculpture garden","MoMA garden free","MoMA 54th Street garden","MoMA outdoor sculpture"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"whitney","name":"The terraces over the High Line","area":"THE WHITNEY","mood":"Blue afternoon on the river","color":"#a8c0d0","description":"Gansevoort Street where the High Line ends: the stepped museum, its outdoor stairs and terraces climbing the east face, the largest column free gallery inside, the river and the works on both.","signatures":"The asymmetric stack of steel and glass with cantilevered terraces stepping down toward the High Line, the outside steel stairs between them, the vast open gallery with its sawtooth skylights, the Hudson and the piers to the west, the cobbled meatpacking streets.","daylit":true,"index":76,"place":"The Whitney Museum of American Art","fact":"Gertrude Vanderbilt Whitney founded the Whitney in 1930 after the Met declined her collection, and it opened on West 8th Street in 1931. The museum moved to Marcel Breuer's building on Madison Avenue in 1966 and to Renzo Piano's building at 99 Gansevoort Street in 2015. The Whitney Biennial, its survey of American art, dates to 1932.","year":"2015","learn":"Founded by a sculptor in 1930, moved to the end of the High Line in 2015.","source":{"name":"Whitney Museum of American Art","url":"https://whitney.org"},"keywords":["Whitney Museum","Whitney Museum of American Art","Whitney Biennial","Renzo Piano Whitney","Whitney High Line","Whitney Gansevoort Street"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"ellis","name":"The registry room","area":"ELLIS ISLAND","mood":"Arrival morning","color":"#c8a888","description":"The ferry slip and the red brick palace with its four towers, then the great hall: the tiled vault, the flags, the long benches, the stair of separation, the works where the inspectors stood.","signatures":"The French Renaissance main building in red brick and limestone with copper domed towers, the registry room with its Guastavino tile vault and three great arched windows, the iron railings, the American flags, the benches in rows.","daylit":true,"index":77,"place":"Ellis Island","fact":"Ellis Island opened as the federal immigration station on January 1, 1892, and Annie Moore of Ireland was the first to pass through. After a fire, the current main building by Boring and Tilton opened in 1900, and the Guastavino tile vault of the Registry Room went in during 1918. About twelve million immigrants passed through before the station closed in 1954, and the museum opened in 1990.","year":"1892 to 1954","learn":"Twelve million people came through this hall between 1892 and 1954.","source":{"name":"National Park Service, Ellis Island","url":"https://www.nps.gov/elis/"},"keywords":["Ellis Island","Ellis Island museum","Ellis Island Registry Room","Ellis Island Great Hall","Ellis Island passenger search","Ellis Island ferry"],"article":"liberty-and-ellis-island","articles":["liberty-and-ellis-island"]},{"id":"marathon","name":"Mile one","area":"THE VERRAZZANO","mood":"First Sunday in November","color":"#3a8ad8","description":"The upper deck of the bridge at the start: fifty thousand runners pouring over the crest toward Brooklyn, the harbor on both sides, the towers, the fireboat below, the works on the tower legs.","signatures":"The two steel towers rising from the Narrows, the upper roadway packed curb to curb with runners in colour, the cables sweeping down, the fireboat spraying, Fort Wadsworth at the Staten Island end, Bay Ridge ahead, helicopters.","daylit":true,"index":78,"place":"The New York City Marathon at the Verrazzano-Narrows Bridge","fact":"The first New York City Marathon in 1970 ran loops of Central Park with 127 starters, organized by Fred Lebow and New York Road Runners. The race went through all five boroughs in 1976 and now starts at Fort Wadsworth on Staten Island. Runners cross the Verrazzano-Narrows Bridge, opened in 1964 and designed by Othmar Ammann, then the longest suspension span in the world.","year":"1970","learn":"Fifty thousand runners cross the bridge in the first mile.","source":{"name":"New York Road Runners","url":"https://www.nyrr.org"},"keywords":["New York City Marathon","NYC Marathon route","Verrazzano Bridge marathon start","TCS New York City Marathon","New York Road Runners","NYC Marathon history"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"halloween","name":"Sixth Avenue after dark","area":"THE VILLAGE HALLOWEEN PARADE","mood":"October 31, eight p.m.","color":"#f08a2a","description":"The avenue closed from Spring to 16th: giant puppets swaying over the crowd, skeletons on poles, drummers, a million people in costume, the works lit on the building fronts.","signatures":"Enormous rod puppets carried above the marchers, skeleton banners, drum corps, floats with lit skulls, spectators ten deep behind barricades, the avenue lit orange from end to end, the Jefferson Market clock tower.","daylit":false,"index":79,"place":"The Village Halloween Parade","fact":"Puppeteer Ralph Lee began the Village Halloween Parade in 1973 as a walk through Greenwich Village for his children and neighbors. Jeanne Fleming has directed it since 1981. It moves up Sixth Avenue from Spring Street to 16th Street with giant puppets, bands and anyone in costume.","year":"1973","learn":"A puppeteer's neighborhood walk in 1973 became a parade of a million.","source":{"name":"Village Halloween Parade","url":"https://halloween-nyc.com"},"keywords":["Village Halloween Parade","NYC Halloween parade","Halloween parade Sixth Avenue","Greenwich Village Halloween","Halloween parade puppets","how to march in the Halloween parade"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"easternparkway","name":"The parkway on Labor Day","area":"GRAND ARMY PLAZA","mood":"Carnival Monday","color":"#f1c531","description":"The triumphal arch at the head of the parkway, the museum and the library beside it, the West Indian parade coming up under the elms: feathered masqueraders, sound trucks, flags of every island, the works on the arch.","signatures":"The Soldiers and Sailors arch with its bronze quadriga, the oval plaza with the fountain, the columned Beaux Arts museum, the library shaped like an open book with its gilded doorway, the six lane parkway with elm malls, the costumes and the trucks.","daylit":true,"index":80,"place":"Grand Army Plaza, Eastern Parkway and the West Indian American Day Carnival","fact":"Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux laid out Eastern Parkway in the 1870s as the first road to be called a parkway. The Soldiers' and Sailors' Arch at Grand Army Plaza, designed by John H. Duncan, was dedicated in 1892. Brooklyn's West Indian American Day Carnival parade has come up the parkway on Labor Day since 1969, after earlier Caribbean carnivals in Harlem.","year":"1969","learn":"Olmsted's first parkway carries the Caribbean carnival every Labor Day.","source":{"name":"NYC Parks, Grand Army Plaza","url":"https://www.nycgovparks.org/parks/grand-army-plaza"},"keywords":["West Indian Day Parade","Labor Day parade Brooklyn","Eastern Parkway carnival","Grand Army Plaza arch","J'ouvert Brooklyn","Eastern Parkway Olmsted"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"manhattanhenge","name":"The sun down 42nd","area":"MANHATTANHENGE","mood":"Two minutes before it drops","color":"#ff9a40","description":"Forty second Street looking west from Tudor City: the sun setting exactly down the axis of the grid, the canyon lit gold, the crowd on the overpass and in the crosswalks, the works catching the last light.","signatures":"The Tudor City overpass packed with people, the Chrysler crown catching the sun to the north, the Grand Central viaduct, the canyon of 42nd running to the Hudson with the sun sitting in it, taxis stopped, every phone up.","daylit":false,"index":81,"place":"Manhattanhenge","fact":"Manhattanhenge is the twice yearly evening when the setting sun lines up with Manhattan's east west streets. The street grid comes from the Commissioners' Plan of 1811 and is rotated about 29 degrees from true east west, which puts the alignments in late May and mid July rather than at the equinoxes. Neil deGrasse Tyson of the Hayden Planetarium named and popularized the event.","year":"1811","learn":"Twice a year the sunset lines up with the 1811 street grid.","source":{"name":"American Museum of Natural History, Hayden Planetarium","url":"https://www.amnh.org/research/hayden-planetarium/manhattanhenge"},"keywords":["Manhattanhenge","Manhattanhenge dates","Manhattanhenge 42nd Street","best place to see Manhattanhenge","Manhattanhenge Tudor City","Neil deGrasse Tyson Manhattanhenge"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"metgreathall","name":"The Great Hall","area":"THE MET","mood":"Saturday at opening","color":"#d8c8a8","description":"Fifth Avenue steps under the MLow banners, then the Great Hall: three domes on their arches, the flower urns, the grand staircase rising to the paintings, the works on the limestone.","signatures":"The Beaux Arts facade with its paired columns and the banners between them, the broad steps and fountains, the vaulted Great Hall with three saucer domes and oculi, the information desk and the five great flower arrangements, the grand staircase.","daylit":true,"index":82,"place":"The Great Hall, The Metropolitan Museum of Art","fact":"The Met was founded in 1870 and opened its first building on this site in Central Park in 1880, designed by Calvert Vaux and Jacob Wrey Mould. Richard Morris Hunt designed the Fifth Avenue facade and the Great Hall, completed in 1902 after his death under his son Richard Howland Hunt. The fresh flowers in the hall's urns are paid for by a bequest from Lila Acheson Wallace.","year":"1902","learn":"Hunt's 1902 hall of three domes, with flowers paid for by a bequest.","source":{"name":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Metropolitan Museum of Art","The Met Great Hall","Met Fifth Avenue","Met museum steps","Met Great Hall flowers","Richard Morris Hunt Met"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"metamerican","name":"The Engelhard Court","area":"THE MET","mood":"Winter light through the wall of glass","color":"#c8d8c8","description":"The glass roofed court on the park side: a bank facade brought indoors, the Tiffany loggia, bronzes among the plantings, the balcony, the works around the marble floor.","signatures":"The sloping glass wall onto the park, the neoclassical marble bank facade standing inside, the loggia of columns with its glass mosaic, the bronze figures and fountains, the mezzanine balcony with its balustrade.","daylit":true,"index":83,"place":"The Charles Engelhard Court, The American Wing, The Met","fact":"The Met's American Wing opened in 1924. The Charles Engelhard Court, a glass roofed garden court by Kevin Roche John Dinkeloo and Associates, opened in 1980 and was renovated in 2009. Its centerpiece is the marble facade of the Branch Bank of the United States, built on Wall Street in 1824 and moved to the museum, with a loggia by Louis Comfort Tiffany from his estate Laurelton Hall.","year":"1980","learn":"A Wall Street bank front from 1824 stands inside a glass court.","source":{"name":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Met American Wing","Charles Engelhard Court","Engelhard Court Met","Tiffany loggia Met","Met American Wing courtyard","Branch Bank of the United States facade"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"meteuropean","name":"The skylit suite","area":"THE MET","mood":"Museum daylight","color":"#b8a888","description":"The second floor galleries in a line: skylights with their diffusers, red and grey walls, oak floors, benches in the middle, the works hung at the museum's own height.","signatures":"The enfilade of galleries seen through aligned doorways, the coved skylights with their scrim, dark red and slate walls, herringbone oak floors, leather benches, the labels, the guards at the doors.","daylit":true,"index":84,"place":"The European Paintings galleries, The Met","fact":"The Met hangs its European paintings in a long run of skylit galleries on the second floor above the Great Hall. The collection holds five paintings by Vermeer, along with Rembrandt, El Greco, Velazquez, Bruegel and the Impressionists. A five year project to replace the skylights finished in 2023, and the galleries reopened rehung under the new light.","year":"2023","learn":"Five Vermeers hang under skylights rebuilt in 2023.","source":{"name":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Met European Paintings","Met Vermeer","Met skylight galleries","Met second floor paintings","European paintings 1300 to 1800 Met","Met Old Masters"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"oceanlife","name":"Under the whale","area":"NATURAL HISTORY","mood":"Deep blue afternoon","color":"#3a6aa8","description":"The two storey hall lit like the sea floor, the blue whale hanging over everything, the dioramas glowing in the walls, the works between the windows of the deep.","signatures":"The ninety four foot whale suspended from the ceiling, the blue light and the ocean floor mural, the upper and lower tiers of dioramas set into the walls, the balcony, the great hall doorway with its columns.","daylit":true,"index":85,"place":"The Milstein Hall of Ocean Life, American Museum of Natural History","fact":"The Hall of Ocean Life opened at the American Museum of Natural History in 1933. The 94 foot fiberglass blue whale model was hung from the ceiling in 1969, and the hall was renovated and renamed for the Milstein family in 2003. Dioramas of sea life line the walls on two levels beneath the whale.","year":"1969","learn":"A 94 foot whale has hung here since 1969.","source":{"name":"American Museum of Natural History","url":"https://www.amnh.org"},"keywords":["Milstein Hall of Ocean Life","blue whale Natural History Museum","AMNH blue whale","Hall of Ocean Life","American Museum of Natural History whale","sleepover under the whale"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"rosecenter","name":"The sphere in the cube","area":"THE ROSE CENTER","mood":"Space show letting out","color":"#8ab8e8","description":"The glass cube on 81st Street with the great sphere floating inside, the spiral walkway of cosmic time, the planets on their orbits, the works on the balconies at every level.","signatures":"The six storey glass cube, the eighty seven foot sphere held in its cradle, the ramp spiralling round it, the scale models of planets hanging in the void, the granite terraces outside, the night lit interior.","daylit":true,"index":86,"place":"The Rose Center for Earth and Space, American Museum of Natural History","fact":"The Rose Center for Earth and Space opened in February 2000 on 81st Street, designed by James Stewart Polshek's firm. An 87 foot sphere holding the Hayden Planetarium floats inside a glass cube. The original Hayden Planetarium had opened on the site in 1935.","year":"2000","learn":"An 87 foot sphere in a glass cube, opened in 2000.","source":{"name":"American Museum of Natural History","url":"https://www.amnh.org"},"keywords":["Rose Center for Earth and Space","Hayden Planetarium","AMNH planetarium","glass cube 81st Street","Rose Center sphere","Hayden Planetarium show"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"momaatrium","name":"The atrium","area":"MOMA","mood":"Free Friday, six o'clock","color":"#e8e8e4","description":"Fifty third Street under the black glass, the lobby, then the atrium: a white shaft of light six storeys tall, bridges crossing it, the escalators, a mobile turning above, the works on the great walls.","signatures":"The sheer white atrium with its cut through balconies at every level, the glass bridges, the grand stair, the mobile hung in the void, the escalators seen through glass, the garden through the wall of windows.","daylit":true,"index":87,"place":"The Museum of Modern Art","fact":"MoMA was founded in 1929 by Lillie P. Bliss, Mary Quinn Sullivan and Abby Aldrich Rockefeller. Its 53rd Street building by Philip Goodwin and Edward Durell Stone opened in 1939. Yoshio Taniguchi's expansion opened in 2004 with a 110 foot atrium at the center, and Diller Scofidio and Renfro's expansion followed in 2019.","year":"2004","learn":"Three women founded it in 1929; the atrium came in 2004.","source":{"name":"The Museum of Modern Art","url":"https://www.moma.org"},"keywords":["MoMA","Museum of Modern Art","MoMA 53rd Street","MoMA atrium","MoMA Taniguchi building","MoMA tickets"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"newmuseum","name":"The stacked boxes","area":"THE NEW MUSEUM","mood":"Bowery, late afternoon","color":"#c8ccd0","description":"The Bowery with its restaurant supply stores, then the seven shifted boxes in silver mesh, the tall gallery with the light slot, the sky room at the top, the works on raw white walls.","signatures":"The stack of aluminium mesh clad boxes each shifted off the one below, the slot windows where the shifts open, the double height gallery with a skylight strip, the green elevator, the sky room with its wraparound terrace.","daylit":true,"index":88,"place":"The New Museum","fact":"Marcia Tucker founded the New Museum in 1977 to show living artists. Its building on the Bowery, seven shifted boxes wrapped in aluminum mesh, was designed by Kazuyo Sejima and Ryue Nishizawa of SANAA and opened in 2007. An expansion by OMA next door opened in 2025.","year":"2007","learn":"Seven stacked boxes on the Bowery, opened in 2007.","source":{"name":"New Museum","url":"https://www.newmuseum.org"},"keywords":["New Museum","New Museum Bowery","SANAA New Museum","New Museum expansion","New Museum sky room","contemporary art museum Bowery"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"neuegalerie","name":"The gold room","area":"NEUE GALERIE","mood":"Café hour","color":"#d8b060","description":"The mansion at 86th and Fifth, the marble stair with its bronze rail, the second floor salon where the gold portrait hangs, the café below with its marble tables and newspapers.","signatures":"The Beaux Arts limestone mansion with iron and glass canopy, the spiral marble staircase, the wood panelled galleries with the Vienna 1900 furniture, the café with bentwood chairs and a display of cakes.","daylit":true,"index":89,"place":"Neue Galerie New York","fact":"Ronald Lauder and Serge Sabarsky founded the Neue Galerie for German and Austrian art, and it opened in 2001 in the William Starr Miller mansion at 1048 Fifth Avenue, built in 1914 by Carrere and Hastings. In 2006 Lauder bought Gustav Klimt's 1907 Portrait of Adele Bloch-Bauer I after its restitution to Maria Altmann. Cafe Sabarsky on the ground floor serves Viennese food.","year":"2001","learn":"Klimt's gold portrait hangs in a 1914 Fifth Avenue mansion.","source":{"name":"Neue Galerie New York","url":"https://www.neuegalerie.org"},"keywords":["Neue Galerie","Neue Galerie Klimt","Woman in Gold painting","Adele Bloch-Bauer","Cafe Sabarsky","Neue Galerie 86th Street"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"cooperhewitt","name":"The Carnegie garden","area":"COOPER HEWITT","mood":"Garden party","color":"#a8c090","description":"The mansion on 91st Street from its own lawn: the conservatory, the terrace and pergola, the great hall with its carved oak stair, the works in the design galleries and out on the garden walls.","signatures":"The Georgian brick and limestone mansion set back behind a lawn and iron fence, the glass and bronze conservatory, the Scottish oak entrance hall with organ pipes, the garden pergola of wisteria, the terrace where the summer parties happen.","daylit":true,"index":90,"place":"Cooper Hewitt, Smithsonian Design Museum","fact":"The Hewitt sisters founded the museum in 1897 as part of Cooper Union, and it joined the Smithsonian in 1967. Since 1976 it has occupied Andrew Carnegie's mansion on 91st Street, built between 1899 and 1902 by Babb, Cook and Willard, with its own garden. It is the only museum in the United States devoted to historic and contemporary design.","year":"1976","learn":"Andrew Carnegie's mansion, a design museum since 1976.","source":{"name":"Cooper Hewitt, Smithsonian Design Museum","url":"https://www.cooperhewitt.org"},"keywords":["Cooper Hewitt","Cooper Hewitt Design Museum","Carnegie Mansion","Cooper Hewitt garden","Smithsonian design museum New York","Cooper Hewitt 91st Street"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"breuer","name":"The inverted ziggurat","area":"THE BREUER","mood":"Granite grey noon","color":"#8a8a88","description":"Madison at 75th: the granite building stepping out over the moat, the bridge in, the concrete coffered ceilings, the trapezoid window, the works in the great fourth floor gallery.","signatures":"The three cantilevered granite storeys each overhanging the one below, the sunken sculpture court and the concrete bridge over it, the single angled window on the facade, the bush hammered concrete inside, the bluestone floors, the grid of circular ceiling lights.","daylit":true,"index":91,"place":"945 Madison Avenue, the Breuer building","fact":"Marcel Breuer designed the granite building at 945 Madison Avenue with Hamilton Smith, and it opened in 1966 as the Whitney Museum. The Met ran it as the Met Breuer from 2016 to 2020, and the Frick showed its collection there from 2021 to 2024 while its mansion was renovated. Sotheby's bought the building in 2023.","year":"1966","learn":"Breuer's 1966 granite ziggurat has housed three museums and an auction house.","source":{"name":"Whitney Museum of American Art","url":"https://whitney.org"},"keywords":["Breuer building","945 Madison Avenue","Met Breuer","Frick Madison","Marcel Breuer Whitney","Sotheby's Breuer building"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"jewishmuseum","name":"The Warburg mansion","area":"THE JEWISH MUSEUM","mood":"Sunday on Museum Mile","color":"#b8a8c8","description":"The French Gothic mansion at 92nd and Fifth, the carved limestone and the copper roofs, the panelled rooms inside stacked with galleries, the works among the silver and the paintings.","signatures":"The Gothic Revival chateau in grey limestone with pointed dormers and a slate roof, the arched entrance with tracery, the oak panelled rooms of the old house, the white modern galleries above, the Fifth Avenue trees.","daylit":true,"index":92,"place":"The Jewish Museum","fact":"The Jewish Museum began in 1904 with a gift of objects to the Jewish Theological Seminary. Since 1947 it has occupied the French Gothic mansion at 92nd Street and Fifth Avenue built in 1908 for Felix and Frieda Warburg, designed by C. P. H. Gilbert. Kevin Roche's expansion in 1993 extended the mansion's limestone facade, and the collection holds about 30,000 objects.","year":"1947","learn":"The Warburg mansion of 1908 has held the museum since 1947.","source":{"name":"The Jewish Museum","url":"https://thejewishmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Jewish Museum New York","Warburg mansion","Jewish Museum Fifth Avenue","Jewish Museum 92nd Street","Jewish Museum exhibitions","Felix Warburg house"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"rubin","name":"The spiral stair","area":"THE RUBIN","mood":"Incense and steel","color":"#c86a3a","description":"Seventeenth Street off Seventh, the six floors turning around a marble and steel spiral, the galleries in deep red and saffron, the works climbing with the stair.","signatures":"The steel and marble spiral staircase inherited from the department store, the circular void it climbs through, the dark saturated gallery walls, the shrine room lit by butter lamps, the cafe at the bottom.","daylit":false,"index":93,"place":"The Rubin Museum of Art","fact":"Donald and Shelley Rubin opened the Rubin Museum in 2004 in the former Barneys New York store at 150 West 17th Street, keeping the spiral staircase Andree Putman had designed for the store. The museum showed art of the Himalayas, Tibet and India. It closed its building in October 2024 to continue as a museum without walls, lending and touring its collection.","year":"2004","learn":"Himalayan art on a spiral stair inherited from Barneys.","source":{"name":"Rubin Museum of Art","url":"https://rubinmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Rubin Museum","Rubin Museum of Art","Himalayan art museum New York","Rubin Museum spiral staircase","Rubin Museum closing","Tibetan art New York"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"theshed","name":"The shell rolls out","area":"THE SHED","mood":"Opening night at Hudson Yards","color":"#8ab0d0","description":"The telescoping shell on its rails rolling out over the plaza to make the McCourt, the pillow skin lit from within, the works inside the hall and up on the gallery levels.","signatures":"The quilted translucent shell on giant wheels sliding on rails out from the base building, the McCourt hall it encloses, the exposed steel diagrid, the plaza and the Vessel beyond, the High Line spur.","daylit":false,"index":94,"place":"The Shed","fact":"The Shed opened in April 2019 at Hudson Yards, designed by Diller Scofidio and Renfro with Rockwell Group. Its outer shell, clad in translucent plastic cushions, rolls out on rails over the plaza to enclose a hall called the McCourt. The building commissions new work across performance, visual art and music.","year":"2019","learn":"The building's shell rolls out on rails to make a hall.","source":{"name":"The Shed","url":"https://theshed.org"},"keywords":["The Shed Hudson Yards","The Shed NYC","McCourt Shed","Shed telescoping shell","Diller Scofidio Renfro Shed","The Shed tickets"],"article":"high-line-rewilded-city","articles":["high-line-rewilded-city"]},{"id":"armory","name":"The drill hall","area":"PARK AVENUE ARMORY","mood":"An installation at dusk","color":"#8a6a4a","description":"The brick fortress on Park Avenue, the period rooms of the regiment, then the drill hall: fifty five thousand square feet under iron arches, the works standing in the dark like an installation.","signatures":"The Gothic Revival brick armory with its crenellated towers, the Tiffany and Herter Brothers company rooms, the drill hall's wrought iron barrel trusses eighty feet high, the vast wooden floor, the balcony round the hall.","daylit":false,"index":95,"place":"Park Avenue Armory","fact":"The Seventh Regiment Armory on Park Avenue was built between 1877 and 1881 to designs by Charles W. Clinton, with interiors by Louis Comfort Tiffany, Stanford White and the Herter Brothers. Its drill hall spans 55,000 square feet under iron arches. A conservancy took over the building in 2007 and runs it as a venue for large scale art and performance.","year":"1881","learn":"Tiffany rooms and a drill hall of 55,000 square feet, built 1881.","source":{"name":"Park Avenue Armory","url":"https://www.armoryonpark.org"},"keywords":["Park Avenue Armory","Seventh Regiment Armory","Wade Thompson Drill Hall","Park Avenue Armory Tiffany rooms","Park Avenue Armory events","67th Street armory"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"customhouse","name":"The rotunda at Bowling Green","area":"THE CUSTOM HOUSE","mood":"Harbor light through the skylight","color":"#c8b8a0","description":"Bowling Green and the four continents on their pedestals, the granite palace of the old Custom House, then the oval rotunda: a tile dome that floats with no columns, the murals of the harbor, the works in the collector's offices.","signatures":"The Beaux Arts granite front with its giant columns and the four seated continents, the elliptical rotunda with a Guastavino tile dome and skylight, the harbor murals in the dome's spandrels, the old collector's counters in marble and bronze.","daylit":true,"index":96,"place":"The Alexander Hamilton U.S. Custom House","fact":"Cass Gilbert designed the Custom House at Bowling Green, built between 1902 and 1907, with Daniel Chester French's four seated figures of the continents at the entrance. The oval rotunda has a Guastavino tile dome and murals by Reginald Marsh painted in 1937. Since 1994 the building has housed the Smithsonian's National Museum of the American Indian.","year":"1907","learn":"Cass Gilbert's 1907 rotunda, painted by Reginald Marsh in 1937.","source":{"name":"National Museum of the American Indian","url":"https://americanindian.si.edu"},"keywords":["Custom House Bowling Green","Alexander Hamilton Custom House","National Museum of the American Indian New York","Cass Gilbert Custom House","Four Continents statues","Reginald Marsh rotunda murals"],"article":"consolidation-1898","articles":["consolidation-1898","counting-new-york","before-the-city-lenape-new-amsterdam"]},{"id":"mad","name":"The lollipop building","area":"MUSEUM OF ARTS AND DESIGN","mood":"Columbus Circle at rush hour","color":"#e8e0d0","description":"The circle with its column and fountains, the white tower with the slot windows cut through it in a line, the tall galleries inside, the works in glass and terracotta light.","signatures":"The nine storey tower on the circle sheathed in white glazed terracotta and glass, the vertical slot of windows snaking up its face, the circle with the column and monument and the ring of fountains, the park behind.","daylit":true,"index":97,"place":"The Museum of Arts and Design","fact":"Aileen Osborn Webb founded the museum in 1956 as the Museum of Contemporary Crafts. Its building at 2 Columbus Circle was built in 1964 by Edward Durell Stone for Huntington Hartford's Gallery of Modern Art and was nicknamed the lollipop building for its ground floor columns. Brad Cloepfil of Allied Works reclad it, and the museum opened there in 2008.","year":"2008","learn":"The lollipop building at Columbus Circle, reclad in 2008.","source":{"name":"Museum of Arts and Design","url":"https://madmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Museum of Arts and Design","MAD museum","2 Columbus Circle","lollipop building","MAD Columbus Circle","craft museum New York"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"hispanicsociety","name":"The terracotta court","area":"THE HISPANIC SOCIETY","mood":"Audubon Terrace, afternoon","color":"#c8784a","description":"The Beaux Arts terrace on Broadway at 155th, the bronze horseman, then the two storey court in red terracotta with its arcaded gallery, the works among the Spanish painters.","signatures":"The plaza of white limestone institutions with the bronze equestrian at its centre, the main court lined in ornate red Spanish Renaissance terracotta with an arcaded upper gallery, the skylight, the Sorolla room of vision of Spain.","daylit":true,"index":98,"place":"The Hispanic Society Museum and Library","fact":"Archer Milton Huntington founded the Hispanic Society of America in 1904 and opened its building at Audubon Terrace on Broadway at 155th Street in 1908, designed by his cousin Charles Pratt Huntington. His wife Anna Hyatt Huntington made the bronze El Cid on the terrace. The collection holds Goya's Duchess of Alba and Sorolla's Vision of Spain murals.","year":"1908","learn":"Goya and Sorolla in a terracotta court on 155th Street since 1908.","source":{"name":"Hispanic Society Museum and Library","url":"https://hispanicsociety.org"},"keywords":["Hispanic Society","Hispanic Society Museum and Library","Audubon Terrace","Sorolla Vision of Spain","Goya Duchess of Alba New York","Hispanic Society 155th Street"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"studiomuseum","name":"The new building on 125th","area":"THE STUDIO MUSEUM IN HARLEM","mood":"Opening on 125th Street","color":"#d88a3a","description":"One hundred twenty fifth Street with its music, the new building's stacked concrete terraces and the inverted stoop, the tall gallery inside, the works on the walls of Harlem's museum.","signatures":"The new building's facade of layered concrete frames stepping back, the inverted stoop that opens the ground floor to the street as bleacher seating, the double height gallery, the roof terrace, the avenue with its vendors and the Apollo down the block.","daylit":true,"index":99,"place":"The Studio Museum in Harlem","fact":"The Studio Museum in Harlem was founded in 1968 to show artists of African descent, and its artist in residence program has run since its early years. The museum's first purpose built home on West 125th Street, designed by Adjaye Associates with Cooper Robertson, opened in 2025. Its terraces step down toward the street.","year":"1968","learn":"Founded in 1968, in its first purpose built home since 2025.","source":{"name":"The Studio Museum in Harlem","url":"https://www.studiomuseum.org"},"keywords":["Studio Museum in Harlem","Studio Museum new building","Studio Museum 125th Street","Adjaye Studio Museum","Studio Museum artists in residence","Harlem art museum"],"article":"harlem-renaissance-apollo","articles":["harlem-renaissance-apollo"]},{"id":"elmuseo","name":"The courtyard on Fifth","area":"EL MUSEO DEL BARRIO","mood":"Three Kings Day","color":"#e8a030","description":"Fifth Avenue at 104th, the Heckscher building's courtyard with its fountain and the bandstand, the galleries behind their arches, the parade of camels and kings passing on the avenue, the works in colour.","signatures":"The red brick and limestone building with its arcaded ground floor, the courtyard with a fountain and palms, the ornate theater doors, the Three Kings Day parade with puppets, camels and the paper crowns, the park across the avenue.","daylit":true,"index":100,"place":"El Museo del Barrio","fact":"Raphael Montanez Ortiz founded El Museo del Barrio in 1969 in East Harlem to show Puerto Rican and Latino art. Since 1977 it has occupied part of the Heckscher Building at 1230 Fifth Avenue at 104th Street, and it began its Three Kings Day parade the same year. The parade brings camels and giant puppets through East Harlem each January.","year":"1969","learn":"Born in East Harlem in 1969, with camels on Fifth Avenue every January.","source":{"name":"El Museo del Barrio","url":"https://www.elmuseo.org"},"keywords":["El Museo del Barrio","El Museo Fifth Avenue","Three Kings Day Parade East Harlem","Latino art museum New York","Museum Mile El Museo","El Museo del Barrio 104th Street"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"mcny","name":"The city's museum","area":"MUSEUM OF THE CITY OF NEW YORK","mood":"Museum Mile, the top of the mile","color":"#b8a8a0","description":"The red brick Georgian building at 103rd, the marble rotunda with its twin curving stairs, the timeline gallery, the works telling the city's own story.","signatures":"The Colonial Revival red brick and marble front with its portico and cupola, the entrance rotunda with two sweeping marble staircases, the New York at its core gallery with its wall of screens, the terrace on Fifth.","daylit":true,"index":101,"place":"The Museum of the City of New York","fact":"The Museum of the City of New York was founded in 1923 and first shown at Gracie Mansion. Its Georgian Colonial Revival building on Fifth Avenue at 103rd Street, designed by Joseph H. Freedlander, opened in 1932. The museum collects the city's own history, from Dutch settlement to the present.","year":"1932","learn":"The city's own museum, in a Georgian building since 1932.","source":{"name":"Museum of the City of New York","url":"https://www.mcny.org"},"keywords":["Museum of the City of New York","MCNY","New York at Its Core","MCNY 103rd Street","Museum Mile city museum","New York City history museum"],"article":"consolidation-1898","articles":["consolidation-1898","counting-new-york","before-the-city-lenape-new-amsterdam"]},{"id":"nyhistorical","name":"The oldest museum in the city","area":"NEW-YORK HISTORICAL SOCIETY","mood":"Central Park West, a school morning","color":"#c8b898","description":"The granite temple front across from the park, the great hall of the city's history with its wall of lamps, the library above, the works among the relics.","signatures":"The Roman Eclectic granite facade with its Ionic columns and bronze doors, the entrance hall with the mural and the wall of Tiffany lamps upstairs, the Luce Center of open storage, the reading room with green shaded lamps.","daylit":true,"index":102,"place":"The New York Historical","fact":"The New-York Historical Society was founded in 1804 and is the oldest museum in New York City. Its granite building on Central Park West at 77th Street, designed by York and Sawyer, opened in 1908. The collection includes John James Audubon's watercolors for The Birds of America and a large group of Tiffany lamps.","year":"1804","learn":"The city's oldest museum, founded in 1804.","source":{"name":"New York Historical","url":"https://www.nyhistory.org"},"keywords":["New-York Historical Society","New York Historical museum","oldest museum in New York","Audubon watercolors New-York Historical","Tiffany lamps Central Park West","New York Historical library"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"diachelsea","name":"The long light","area":"DIA CHELSEA","mood":"Twenty second Street, grey day","color":"#a8a8a0","description":"The brick warehouses on West 22nd, the sawtooth roofs, then the joined galleries: bare brick and concrete under skylights the length of a block, the works given all the room they want.","signatures":"Three former marble and truck garages joined into one, the exposed brick walls painted white and left raw, the sawtooth skylights, the poured floors, the loading dock front, the High Line passing behind.","daylit":true,"index":103,"place":"Dia Chelsea","fact":"Dia Art Foundation was founded in 1974 to support ambitious long term projects by artists. Dia opened its first Chelsea exhibition building at 548 West 22nd Street in 1987, years before galleries filled the neighborhood. Its current Dia Chelsea, joined former warehouses across the street at 535 to 541 West 22nd, reopened in 2021 after a renovation by Architecture Research Office.","year":"1987","learn":"Dia came to West 22nd Street in 1987, before the galleries followed.","source":{"name":"Dia Art Foundation","url":"https://www.diaart.org"},"keywords":["Dia Chelsea","Dia Art Foundation","Dia West 22nd Street","Dia Chelsea exhibitions","Dia Beacon and Dia Chelsea","Chelsea art foundation"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"chelseablock","name":"Thursday openings on 24th","area":"WEST 24TH STREET","mood":"Six to eight, plastic cups","color":"#f4f4f0","description":"The block of white cubes between Tenth and Eleventh: garage doors rolled up, the crowds spilling onto the sidewalk with their wine, three galleries open in a row, the works inside each.","signatures":"The low former garages turned galleries with roll up fronts, white cube interiors under fluorescent grids, the desks with the price lists, the crowd on the sidewalk at an opening, the High Line at the corner and the river at the end of the street.","daylit":false,"index":104,"place":"The Chelsea gallery district, West 24th Street","fact":"This room interprets the gallery blocks of West Chelsea between Tenth and Eleventh Avenues, a district that grew after Dia opened on West 22nd Street in 1987 and SoHo rents pushed dealers north in the 1990s. Gagosian opened its West 24th Street gallery in 1999, and Gladstone and other galleries share the block. The city designated the West Chelsea Historic District in 2008.","year":"2008","learn":"Former garages turned into the city's densest run of galleries.","source":{"name":"NYC Landmarks Preservation Commission, West Chelsea Historic District designation report","url":"https://s-media.nyc.gov/agencies/lpc/lp/2302.pdf"},"keywords":["Chelsea galleries","West 24th Street galleries","Chelsea gallery openings Thursday","Gagosian 24th Street","Chelsea art district","West Chelsea Historic District"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"fotografiska","name":"The mission house","area":"FOTOGRAFISKA","mood":"Park Avenue South, after nine","color":"#8a4a5a","description":"The Renaissance Revival landmark at 22nd Street, six floors of dark galleries where the photographs glow, the bar at the top, the works lit like prints in the dark.","signatures":"The 1894 brick and terracotta church missions house with its arched loggia, the black painted galleries with spot lit photographs, the wide stair, the top floor restaurant with its arched windows over the avenue.","daylit":false,"index":105,"place":"Fotografiska New York, the Church Missions House","fact":"The Church Missions House at 281 Park Avenue South was built between 1892 and 1894 to designs by Robert W. Gibson and Edward J. Neville Stent, and the city landmarked it in 1979. Fotografiska, the photography museum founded in Stockholm in 2010, opened its New York branch in the building in December 2019. Its galleries are kept dark so the prints carry the light.","year":"2019","learn":"A photography museum in an 1894 mission house.","source":{"name":"Fotografiska","url":"https://www.fotografiska.com"},"keywords":["Fotografiska New York","Fotografiska Park Avenue South","Church Missions House","photography museum New York","Fotografiska exhibitions","Fotografiska 22nd Street"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"ps1","name":"The old school","area":"MOMA PS1","mood":"Warm Up Saturday","color":"#c8a888","description":"The Romanesque public school in Long Island City, the concrete courtyard with its summer stage and the crowd, the classrooms turned galleries down long corridors, the boiler room, the works in the old school's bones.","signatures":"The Victorian brick schoolhouse with its tower, the walled concrete courtyard with the summer installation and DJ stage, the long corridors of classrooms with their original wood and radiators, the sky room with the roof cut open.","daylit":true,"index":106,"place":"MoMA PS1","fact":"Alanna Heiss founded the Institute for Art and Urban Resources in 1971 and in 1976 opened P.S.1 in a Romanesque Revival public school built in 1892 in Long Island City. The classrooms became galleries, and James Turrell cut his skyspace Meeting into the building in 1986. It joined MoMA in 2000, and its summer Warm Up parties fill the courtyard.","year":"1976","learn":"An 1892 public school turned into galleries in 1976.","source":{"name":"MoMA PS1","url":"https://www.momaps1.org"},"keywords":["MoMA PS1","PS1 Long Island City","PS1 Warm Up","James Turrell Meeting PS1","PS1 Queens museum","Alanna Heiss PS1"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"noguchi","name":"The stone garden","area":"THE NOGUCHI MUSEUM","mood":"Vernon Boulevard, still","color":"#8a8a80","description":"A block of industrial Queens, the concrete block building and the walled garden: basalt and granite standing in gravel under a katsura tree, the open air gallery, the works among the stones.","signatures":"The plain concrete block and brick factory with its open roofed first gallery, the garden of stones on raked gravel with bamboo and a katsura, the water sculpture, the paper lanterns hung inside, the raw light.","daylit":true,"index":107,"place":"The Noguchi Museum","fact":"Isamu Noguchi designed and opened his own museum in 1985 in a converted factory building across from his Long Island City studio. The galleries and walled garden hold his stone, metal and wood sculpture, his Akari light sculptures and models for his gardens and playgrounds. He died in 1988, and the museum stays as he arranged it.","year":"1985","learn":"A sculptor built his own museum around a garden in 1985.","source":{"name":"The Noguchi Museum","url":"https://www.noguchi.org"},"keywords":["Noguchi Museum","Isamu Noguchi Museum Queens","Noguchi garden","Noguchi Museum Long Island City","Akari lamps Noguchi","Noguchi sculpture garden"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"socrates","name":"Sculpture on the river","area":"SOCRATES SCULPTURE PARK","mood":"Wind off Hallets Cove","color":"#7aa07a","description":"The reclaimed lot on the East River: big sculpture on the lawn, the cove and the island across the water, kayaks pulled up, the works standing in the open air.","signatures":"The open waterfront lawn with monumental outdoor sculpture, the wooden studio shed and gate of found steel, the rocky shore and kayak launch, Roosevelt Island and the Manhattan skyline across the river.","daylit":true,"index":108,"place":"Socrates Sculpture Park","fact":"Sculptor Mark di Suvero led a group of artists and neighbors who turned an abandoned landfill on the East River in Long Island City into Socrates Sculpture Park in 1986. It became a city park in 1998. It shows large outdoor sculpture and stays free and open every day.","year":"1986","learn":"Artists cleared a dump on the East River in 1986 and made a park.","source":{"name":"Socrates Sculpture Park","url":"https://socratessculpturepark.org"},"keywords":["Socrates Sculpture Park","Socrates Sculpture Park Queens","Mark di Suvero park","outdoor sculpture park Long Island City","Socrates park events","East River sculpture park"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"brooklynmuseum","name":"The Beaux-Arts Court","area":"THE BROOKLYN MUSEUM","mood":"First Saturday","color":"#c8b8a0","description":"The glass pavilion and the great stepped plaza with its fountain, then the third floor court: glass floor, columns on two levels, the skylight, the works around the balcony.","signatures":"The McKim Mead and White facade with its columns and the modern glass entrance pavilion, the fanned plaza steps and the jet fountains, the Beaux-Arts Court with its translucent glass floor, arcaded gallery and skylight, the museum's Rodin bronzes.","daylit":true,"index":109,"place":"The Brooklyn Museum","fact":"The Brooklyn Museum traces its origins to the Brooklyn Apprentices' Library of 1823. Its Beaux Arts building by McKim, Mead and White opened in 1897, and the glass entrance pavilion by Polshek Partnership opened in 2004. The museum is known for its Egyptian collection and holds Judy Chicago's The Dinner Party.","year":"1897","learn":"McKim, Mead and White's 1897 palace on Eastern Parkway.","source":{"name":"Brooklyn Museum","url":"https://www.brooklynmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Brooklyn Museum","Brooklyn Museum Beaux-Arts Court","Brooklyn Museum Egyptian art","The Dinner Party Brooklyn Museum","Brooklyn Museum First Saturdays","Eastern Parkway museum"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"pioneerworks","name":"The ironworks in Red Hook","area":"PIONEER WORKS","mood":"Second Sunday, the garden open","color":"#a88a6a","description":"Pioneer Street near the water: the 1866 iron works building, three storeys of open floor around the great hall, the garden with its kiln and stage, the works hung on brick under the trusses.","signatures":"The long brick ironworks with its arched windows, the triple height main hall with balconies on three sides, the timber trusses, the garden of raised beds and a stage, the Statue and the harbor at the end of the street.","daylit":true,"index":110,"place":"Pioneer Works","fact":"Artist Dustin Yellin founded Pioneer Works in 2012 in a three story iron works building from 1866 on Pioneer Street in Red Hook, Brooklyn. The center hosts residencies, exhibitions, science programs and concerts in its open hall and garden. Admission is free.","year":"2012","learn":"An 1866 iron works in Red Hook, an art center since 2012.","source":{"name":"Pioneer Works","url":"https://pioneerworks.org"},"keywords":["Pioneer Works","Pioneer Works Red Hook","Dustin Yellin Pioneer Works","Pioneer Works Second Sundays","Red Hook art center","Pioneer Iron Works building"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"bronxmuseum","name":"The Concourse museum","area":"THE BRONX MUSEUM OF THE ARTS","mood":"Grand Concourse, Friday","color":"#c8a0b8","description":"The Grand Concourse with its Art Deco apartment houses, the folded metal and glass front of the museum, the atrium and the galleries inside, the works of the borough on the walls.","signatures":"The pleated aluminium and glass facade folding along the Concourse, the tall lobby with its stair, the flexible white galleries, the north wing courtyard, the Deco buildings across the boulevard with their casement corners.","daylit":true,"index":111,"place":"The Bronx Museum of the Arts","fact":"The Bronx Museum of the Arts was founded in 1971 and moved in 1982 into a former synagogue at 1040 Grand Concourse at 165th Street. Arquitectonica's 2006 expansion gave it the folded metal and glass front on the Concourse. The museum focuses on artists of the Bronx and of African, Asian and Latin American descent, and admission is free.","year":"1971","learn":"A free museum on the Grand Concourse since 1982.","source":{"name":"The Bronx Museum of the Arts","url":"https://www.bronxmuseum.org"},"keywords":["Bronx Museum of the Arts","Bronx Museum Grand Concourse","free museum Bronx","Bronx Museum exhibitions","Grand Concourse Art Deco","Bronx Museum 165th Street"],"article":"birth-of-hip-hop-1973","articles":["birth-of-hip-hop-1973"]},{"id":"pneumatic","name":"Letters through the void","area":"THE PNEUMATIC TUBE MAIL, 1897 TO 1953","mood":"Every letter in the city at thirty miles an hour","color":"#be854c","description":"A brick sorting rotunda under a copper dome and a skylight, the cast iron tubes converging overhead with brass collars, capsules sliding through, sorting desks and mail sacks below. Portraits become letters to the city.","signatures":"The circular brick rotunda and copper dome with an oculus, ten glass and iron tubes converging on a brass hub, brass capsule collars, radial oak sorting desks with canvas sacks, the departures board, the four faced station clock, a porch on the avenue.","daylit":true,"index":112,"place":"The pneumatic post","fact":"New York's pneumatic tube mail service began on October 15, 1897, and on August 1, 1898 the tubes crossed the Brooklyn Bridge to reach the Brooklyn General Post Office. The first carrier sent through the system held a Bible, a flag and a copy of the Constitution. About 55 miles of cast iron tube linked 23 Manhattan post offices, each carrier holding roughly 500 letters and moving at five carriers a minute. In 1953 Postmaster General Arthur Summerfield cancelled the contract and replaced the tubes with trucks.","year":"1897","learn":"For 56 years New York shot its mail through iron tubes under the streets.","source":{"name":"United States Postal Service, Postal History","url":"https://about.usps.com/who-we-are/postal-history/pneumatic-tubes.pdf"},"keywords":["pneumatic tube mail New York","Brooklyn Bridge mail tubes 1898","pneumatic post 1897","New York Mail and Newspaper Transportation Company","pneumatic tubes 1953"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"garment","name":"The pattern cathedral","area":"SEVENTH AVENUE CUTTING LOFT, 1920S TO 1950S","mood":"North light, the last fitting","color":"#baa9cc","description":"A sawtooth roofed loft off Seventh Avenue: north light through industrial glazing on long cutting tables, bolt racks, dress forms, a Singer row, a freight elevator, a rack rolling the aisle. The New Yorkers hang on the brick piers and over the tables.","signatures":"Six sawtooth bays of north facing glazing, cast iron columns on a maple floor, two rows of long cutting tables under hanging work panels, bolt racks by colour, the Singer row with bobbing needles, rolling garment racks, dress forms, the freight elevator gate, garment racks on the sidewalk outside.","daylit":true,"index":113,"place":"The Garment District","fact":"The Garment District was born from the country's first zoning law, passed on July 25, 1916, which pushed clothing makers off Fifth Avenue into a zone between Ninth Avenue and Broadway from 34th to 42nd Street. The first cooperative garment buildings rose at 494 and 500 Seventh Avenue in 1920. By 1926 the district was the fastest growing construction site in the entire city. In 1987 the city created the Special Garment Center District zoning to protect the manufacturing lofts.","year":"1920","learn":"A 1916 zoning fight pushed the needle trades onto Seventh Avenue.","source":{"name":"Garment District Alliance","url":"https://garmentdistrict.nyc/history"},"keywords":["Garment District history","Seventh Avenue garment lofts","494 and 500 Seventh Avenue 1920","1916 zoning law Fifth Avenue","Special Garment Center District 1987"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"diamondexchange","name":"Forty-seven facets","area":"WEST 47TH STREET, THE DIAMOND DISTRICT","mood":"Midnight appraisal","color":"#a6d7e7","description":"An exchange floor off West 47th Street: dealer booths under green glass shades, loupes and scales, chandeliers turning, jewel niches with lit velvet, a vault standing open on a wall of safe deposit boxes. The New Yorkers hang in gilt, like gems.","signatures":"Two aisles of dealer booths with banker's lamps and glass cases, the central spine of booths back to back, arched jewel niches in red velvet along both walls, two crystal chandeliers turning, walnut panelling and a coffered ceiling, the round vault door open on the safe deposit wall, the lit street of DIAMONDS signs outside.","daylit":false,"index":114,"place":"The Diamond District","fact":"New York's diamond trade began around 1790 on Maiden Lane near the port, where more than 300 jewelry firms filled two blocks by the early 1900s. After the 1929 crash many jewelers moved to Canal Street and the Bowery. In 1923 broker Fenimore Goode began putting up buildings made for the diamond trade on West 47th Street between Fifth and Sixth Avenues, and in the 1940s European Jewish refugees fleeing the Nazis filled them. Today about 90 percent of the diamonds entering the United States pass through the block.","year":"1923","learn":"The diamond trade moved uptown from Maiden Lane by way of the Bowery.","source":{"name":"47th Street Business Improvement District","url":"https://diamonddistrict.org/history"},"keywords":["Diamond District 47th Street history","Maiden Lane jewelers","Canal Street Bowery diamond trade","Fenimore Goode 47th Street 1923","Diamond Dealers Club"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"steamworks","name":"The city exhales","area":"CON EDISON DISTRICT STEAM, SINCE 1882","mood":"Pressure held","color":"#d18561","description":"A cathedral scale boiler house on the East River: three riveted pressure vessels, a catwalk, valve wheels and gauges, steam leaking from the flanges, the orange and white stack through the high window. The New Yorkers hang on the boiler flanks and along the inspection wall.","signatures":"Three riveted horizontal boilers with domed ends and steam drums, the raised catwalk with engineers on it, the long brick and steel inspection wall with caged bulbs, valve wheels and gauge dials, the firebox glow, instanced steam rising from the flanges, the banded stack and skyline through the end window, the shift board of time cards.","daylit":true,"index":115,"place":"The steam system","fact":"Manhattan has been heated by district steam since 1882, when the New York Steam Company began service in Lower Manhattan. Con Edison runs it today as the largest steam system in the country. Six generating stations push up to 8.5 million pounds of steam an hour through 105 miles of mains, serving more than 1,500 customers from the southern tip of Manhattan to 96th Street. About 60 percent of the steam comes from cogeneration alongside electricity.","year":"1882","learn":"Since 1882 Manhattan's radiators have run on steam piped under the street.","source":{"name":"Con Edison","url":"https://www.coned.com/en/about-us/media-center/news/2022/03-23/con-edisons-steam-system-will-become-nycs-hottest-new-clean-energy-solution"},"keywords":["Con Edison steam system","New York Steam Company 1882","Manhattan district steam 105 miles","steam stacks Manhattan","largest steam system in the country"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"newsprint","name":"Tomorrow is wet ink","area":"NEWSPAPER ROW PRESS FLOOR, ABOUT 1890","mood":"The city goes to press","color":"#ded2b5","description":"A Park Row press floor at night: giant paper reels turning, the web running through a line of presses, ink rollers, a composing room of type cases, the copy desk under green shades, a newsstand at the door and the World Building dome through the window. The New Yorkers hang along the press gallery.","signatures":"Three paper reels on cradles feeding a moving web through four press units, ink rollers turning, cast iron columns and pendant lamps, the composing room with sloped type cases and the imposing stone, the copy desk, the ticking wall clock over the end window, the gilt World Building dome outside, the EXTRA newsstand on the sidewalk, the front page board of the census.","daylit":false,"index":116,"place":"Newspaper Row","fact":"In the late 19th century the city's papers crowded onto Park Row facing City Hall. The Tribune Building of 1875, by Richard Morris Hunt at Printing House Square, was among the first with elevators, and the Times built at 41 Park Row in 1889 before leaving for Times Square in 1903. Joseph Pulitzer's World Building at 99 Park Row, by George B. Post, rose 309 feet under a copper dome in 1890 and was the world's tallest building until 1894. It was demolished in 1955 for the Brooklyn Bridge ramps.","year":"1890","learn":"Every big paper in the city once printed on one block facing City Hall.","source":{"name":"6sqft","url":"https://www.6sqft.com/the-history-of-newspaper-row-on-downtowns-park-row/"},"keywords":["Newspaper Row Park Row","World Building 99 Park Row","Tribune Building 1875 Richard Morris Hunt","Printing House Square","Pulitzer Building demolished 1955"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"laundry","name":"Spin cycle for a sleepless city","area":"ROOSEVELT AVENUE, JACKSON HEIGHTS","mood":"Three in the morning","color":"#85b9bf","description":"A 24 hour laundromat under the 7 train at 3 a.m.: a horseshoe of washers and stacked dryers turning behind round glass, folding tables down the middle, fluorescent light on the sidewalk, the works on enamel panels above the machines.","signatures":"The yellow and red LAVANDERIA sign, the OPEN 24 HOURS neon in the window, the horseshoe of front loaders with drums turning, rolling wire baskets, orange plastic chairs at the window, the change machine and the soap vending wall, the TV on its bracket, the el across the avenue.","daylit":false,"index":117,"place":"The laundromat","fact":"The coin laundromat is a New York institution: the place where a new arrival first pays their own way, and where the night shift folds its clothes at three in the morning. The city licenses every one of them through the Department of Consumer Affairs. In February 2015 there were 2,570 licensed coin operated laundries in the five boroughs, a 10 percent drop from a decade earlier. Rising retail rents from Harlem to SoHo had pushed longtime laundromats out of Manhattan, which counted fewer than 400, into the outer boroughs where costs are lower.","year":"2015","learn":"Twenty five hundred coin laundries wash the city, fewer every year.","source":{"name":"Crain's New York Business","url":"https://www.crainsnewyork.com/article/20150315/RETAIL_APPAREL/150319908/once-a-fixture-laundromats-disappear"},"keywords":["New York laundromat","24 hour laundromat Queens","coin laundry New York City","licensed laundries NYC","laundromats disappearing Manhattan","night shift laundromat"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"taxidispatch","name":"Every light is a fare","area":"VERNON BOULEVARD, LONG ISLAND CITY","mood":"Five o'clock shift change","color":"#e1b850","description":"A medallion garage in Long Island City at the five o'clock shift change: roll up doors open to the low sun, a fan of numbered dispatch bays, a cab on the lift, the dispatcher in a glass booth under the radio mast, the works hung one to a bay and along the medallion wall.","signatures":"The corrugated roll up doors, the fan of yellow bay lines on an oil stained floor, hanging bay numbers, yellow cabs with their roof lights blinking, the hydraulic lift, tire stacks, the dispatch booth and its antenna, the Manhattan skyline at the end of the street across the river.","daylit":true,"index":118,"place":"The taxi garage","fact":"Electric cabs first ran on New York streets in 1894, followed in 1907 by 600 French built gasoline cabs painted red and green. In 1937 Mayor LaGuardia introduced the medallion system and hack licenses, in 1967 a state law made every cab yellow, and in 1971 the Taxi and Limousine Commission replaced the NYPD Hack Bureau. Rising Manhattan rents in the 1970s and 80s pushed the fleet garages to Long Island City, near Midtown, both airports and the expressways. Drivers there work 12 hour shifts that change near five o'clock.","year":"1937","learn":"The yellow cab's home base moved to Long Island City when Manhattan rents rose.","source":{"name":"Brownstoner","url":"https://www.brownstoner.com/history/where-to-mister/"},"keywords":["taxi medallion 1937 LaGuardia","yellow cab law 1967","taxi garages Long Island City","Taxi and Limousine Commission 1971","New York taxi history"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"flowercold","name":"Before the flowers wake","area":"WEST 28TH STREET, THE FLOWER DISTRICT","mood":"Four in the morning","color":"#abbba2","description":"A wholesaler's cold room on West 28th Street before dawn: strip curtains at the door, steel racks of buckets, tulips and roses by the hundred under frost coloured light, cold air pooling on the wet floor, the works on white fins floating over the flowers.","signatures":"The green awnings and sidewalk buckets of the wholesalers, the plastic strip curtain, insulated panel walls, black buckets on wire racks, tulips and roses in colour blocks, a hand truck of buckets rolling the aisle, the evaporator fans, the loading dock and the delivery van at the curb.","daylit":false,"index":119,"place":"The Flower District","fact":"Wholesale cut flower dealers began gathering on West 28th Street in the late 1870s. George E. Bradshaw and John R. Hartman opened one of the first wholesale florist houses in 1891 at 53 West 28th Street, and Guttman and Raynor followed in 1894 at 101 West 28th Street. The trade spread from 23rd Street to 34th Street before settling between 25th and 29th Streets. At its height more than 200 wholesalers and suppliers worked the block, through Easter week 1895 and the snowstorm of January 1908.","year":"1891","learn":"The flower wholesalers have held West 28th Street since the 1890s.","source":{"name":"New York's Historic Floral District","url":"https://www.flowermuseum.org/our-history"},"keywords":["Flower District West 28th Street","New York wholesale flower market history","Bradshaw and Hartman 1891","Guttman and Raynor florist 1894","Chelsea flower district cold rooms"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"handball","name":"The fourth wall belongs to us","area":"ALLEN STREET, LOWER EAST SIDE","mood":"Golden hour, last game","color":"#96a7bd","description":"Three one wall handball courts on the Lower East Side at golden hour, the walls scaled up to monuments: low sun raking the concrete, a blue ball between wall and hand, the crowd along the chain link, the tenements and their fire escapes behind, the works as a civic triptych on the walls and portraits along the fence.","signatures":"Three sixteen foot walls with the court lines painted on, the short line and sidelines on asphalt, the chain link fence with its gate, the park benches and the trees, the open hydrant on the sidewalk, tenements with fire escapes behind the walls, a pickup game in the middle court.","daylit":true,"index":120,"place":"The handball court","fact":"Irish immigrants brought hard handball to New York in the late 19th century, and the city made the one wall game its own. A court is just a wall 20 feet wide and 16 feet high with a short line drawn 16 feet out. NYC Parks held its first citywide tournament in 1948 at Heckscher Playground in Central Park, and the U.S. Handball Association formed in 1951 to fix the rules. In January 2000 the city set out to renovate 1,500 of its 2,052 handball courts.","year":"1948","learn":"One wall, one rubber ball and more than 2,000 city courts.","source":{"name":"NYC Parks","url":"https://www.nycgovparks.org/parks/barrier-playground/history"},"keywords":["one wall handball New York","NYC Parks handball courts 2,052","Coney Island handball courts","Heckscher Playground handball tournament 1948","Handball Haven Barrier Playground"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"saltvault","name":"Winter stored as a mountain","area":"SPRING STREET AND WEST STREET","mood":"The city before snow","color":"#c6d4e1","description":"The Spring Street Salt Shed: a faceted concrete crystal on the Hudson beside the sanitation garage in its skin of fins. Inside, five thousand tons of salt as a white mountain under the folded shell, plow blades against the wall, a loader idling, the works on the promenade around the pile.","signatures":"The tilted concrete facets cantilevering over the base, the truck door on Spring Street, the garage next door wrapped in perforated fins, the Hudson and the Jersey shore behind, the salt cone with grains sliding, the yellow plow blades, a salt spreader rolling past on West Street, gulls.","daylit":true,"index":121,"place":"The salt shed","fact":"The Spring Street Salt Shed was completed in 2015 for the New York City Department of Sanitation at the foot of Canal Street on the Hudson River. Dattner Architects designed it with WXY architecture and urban design as a faceted concrete crystal rising nearly 70 feet, the cast in place concrete serving as both structure and finish. Inside it holds 5,000 tons of road salt. Across Spring Street the Manhattan Districts 1, 2 and 5 Garage houses more than 150 sanitation vehicles and earned LEED Gold.","year":"2015","learn":"A concrete salt crystal on the Hudson holds 5,000 tons of winter.","source":{"name":"urbanNext","url":"https://urbannext.net/spring-street-salt-shed/"},"keywords":["Spring Street Salt Shed","Dattner Architects WXY salt shed","Department of Sanitation salt shed 2015","5,000 tons road salt Manhattan","Manhattan Districts 1 2 5 Garage"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"katz","name":"Katz’s: The portrait lunch","area":"KATZ’S DELICATESSEN / LOWER EAST SIDE","mood":"Houston Street, lunchtime","color":"#b8262a","description":"The corner of Houston and Ludlow since 1888: the red sign band, salamis in the window, a ticket at the door, the long cutting counter with its numbered stations, and the wall of regulars, every one of them a painted New Yorker.","signatures":"The corner sign band and neon, salamis hung in the windows, the ticket at the door, the numbered cutting stations, steam tables, the tin ceiling and ceiling fans, the photo wall of regulars, the table where Harry met Sally.","daylit":true,"index":122,"place":"Katz's Delicatessen","fact":"Katz's began in 1888 as Iceland Brothers, a small deli on Ludlow Street on the Lower East Side. Willy Katz arrived in 1903 and the name became Iceland and Katz; his cousin Benny bought out the brothers in 1910 and Harry Tarowsky joined the partnership in April 1917. Subway construction pushed the deli across the street to 205 East Houston, where the present storefront went up between 1946 and 1949. During World War II the owners' care packages to their sons became the slogan: Send a salami to your boy in the army. Martin Dell, his son Alan and son in law Fred Austin bought in on the 100th anniversary in 1988.","year":"1888","learn":"The Lower East Side deli that started as Iceland Brothers in 1888.","source":{"name":"Katz's Delicatessen, Our Story","url":"https://katzsdelicatessen.com/our-story"},"keywords":["Katz's Delicatessen","Katz's Deli history","Iceland Brothers 1888","205 East Houston Street","send a salami to your boy in the army","oldest deli in New York"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"barney","name":"Barney Greengrass: Sunday regulars","area":"BARNEY GREENGRASS / UPPER WEST SIDE","mood":"A table near the window","color":"#2f6b46","description":"The Sturgeon King on Amsterdam Avenue since 1929: green awnings under the red and blue sign, sidewalk tables behind the green rail, the appetizing counter with its boards, and the dining room where the regulars now hang where the murals were.","signatures":"The three green awnings and the sign band, the sidewalk rail, the terrazzo, the green wainscot and pale walls, the glass appetizing case with its caviar and herring boards, the pass through to the dining room, the wall clock, the pendant globes.","daylit":true,"index":123,"place":"Barney Greengrass","fact":"Barney Greengrass opened his appetizing store in Harlem in 1908 and moved it to 541 Amsterdam Avenue in 1929. State senator and Tammany Hall leader James J. Frawley coined the title The Sturgeon King, and in 1939 the store shipped smoked sturgeon to President Franklin D. Roosevelt. A restaurant section opened in 1938. Barney's son Moe took over in 1955 and grandson Gary in 1982, and the store turned 100 in 2008.","year":"1929","learn":"A Tammany boss named him the Sturgeon King and the title stuck.","source":{"name":"Barney Greengrass, Our History","url":"https://www.barneygreengrass.com/pages/new-history"},"keywords":["Barney Greengrass","the Sturgeon King","541 Amsterdam Avenue","Upper West Side appetizing store","Barney Greengrass history","smoked sturgeon New York"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"astorhall","name":"Between the lions","area":"NYPL / ASTOR HALL","mood":"Fifth Avenue, mid afternoon","color":"#e4ddcf","description":"Fifth Avenue, Patience and Fortitude on their plinths, the readers on the steps, the three arched portals, then Astor Hall: white Vermont marble under a vault, twin stair flights to the landings, bronze candelabra, the rotunda beyond.","signatures":"The Beaux Arts marble front with paired Corinthian columns between three round portals, the two marble lions flanking the broad steps, the vaulted white marble entrance hall with its lateral stair flights and bronze torcheres.","daylit":true,"index":124,"place":"Astor Hall, New York Public Library","fact":"Astor Hall is the marble entrance lobby of the New York Public Library at 476 Fifth Avenue, the Stephen A. Schwarzman Building. Carrere and Hastings won the design competition in 1897 and the library opened on May 24, 1911. Busts of both architects stand in niches at the foot of the stairs that flank the hall. In November 1974 Astor Hall, the central stairs and the McGraw Rotunda became New York City's first interior landmark.","year":"1911","learn":"The marble lobby that became New York's first interior landmark in 1974.","source":{"name":"NYC Landmarks Preservation Commission, LP-2592","url":"https://s-media.nyc.gov/agencies/lpc/lp/2592.pdf"},"keywords":["Astor Hall","New York Public Library main branch","Stephen A. Schwarzman Building","Carrere and Hastings","first interior landmark New York","476 Fifth Avenue"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"jefferson","name":"The clock keeps stories","area":"JEFFERSON MARKET LIBRARY","mood":"Village Gothic, a bright morning","color":"#be8b75","description":"Sixth Avenue at Tenth Street: the polychrome brick tower with its working clock, the stair turret, the garden through the fence, then the Gothic reading room under timber trusses, stained glass in the pointed windows, green lamps on the tables, the stacks.","signatures":"Victorian Gothic in red brick banded with limestone, the clock tower with its steep pyramid roof and four faces, the round stair turret, pointed arches everywhere, the timber trussed reading room, the garden on the old prison site.","daylit":true,"index":125,"place":"Jefferson Market Library","fact":"The Jefferson Market Courthouse rose at Sixth Avenue and West 10th Street between 1874 and 1877, designed by Calvert Vaux and Frederick Clarke Withers in red brick and limestone. An 1880s poll of architects voted it the fifth most beautiful building in the United States. After the courts left, preservationist Margot Gayle led a Village campaign to save it and restart its four faced clock. Architect Giorgio Cavaglieri converted it into a New York Public Library branch in 1967.","year":"1967","learn":"A Victorian Gothic courthouse the Village saved and turned into a library.","source":{"name":"Village Preservation","url":"https://villagepreservation.org/2012/02/01/the-jefferson-market-library-a-striking-landmark-shines-again/"},"keywords":["Jefferson Market Library","Jefferson Market Courthouse","Vaux and Withers","Margot Gayle","Greenwich Village clock tower","Giorgio Cavaglieri"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"eldridge","name":"An immigrant constellation","area":"ELDRIDGE STREET SYNAGOGUE","mood":"Candle light under painted stars","color":"#819cbd","description":"A narrow Lower East Side street of tenements, the Moorish facade with its wheel window and horseshoe doors, then the sanctuary: a vault of gold stars on blue, the women's gallery on slender columns, brass chandeliers swaying, the round blue window over the ark.","signatures":"The 1887 Moorish Revival front with the rose window and horseshoe arches, the barrel vaulted sanctuary painted with stars, the horseshoe arcaded galleries, the central bimah and the ark, the round east window of blue glass and gold stars, the pews.","daylit":true,"index":126,"place":"Eldridge Street Synagogue","fact":"The Eldridge Street Synagogue opened in 1887 at 12 Eldridge Street, the first synagogue in America purpose built by immigrants from Eastern Europe. After the 1924 immigration quotas the congregation shrank, moved into the lower chapel in the 1940s and left the main sanctuary to decay. It was rediscovered in 1986, named a National Historic Landmark in 1996, and restored by December 2007. A stained glass window by Kiki Smith and Deborah Gans was added in 2010.","year":"1887","learn":"A grand 1887 sanctuary sealed for decades and rediscovered in 1986.","source":{"name":"Museum at Eldridge Street","url":"https://www.eldridgestreet.org/history/"},"keywords":["Eldridge Street Synagogue","Museum at Eldridge Street","12 Eldridge Street","Kiki Smith window","Lower East Side synagogue history","Eastern European Jewish immigrants"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"cityhallrotunda","name":"The public portrait","area":"CITY HALL ROTUNDA","mood":"Civic daylight","color":"#d4c4aa","description":"City Hall Park and its fountain, the marble front under the cupola, then the rotunda: twin marble stairs sweeping up to a ring of ten Corinthian columns under a coffered dome, the Governor's Room through its door, the portraits of the city between the columns.","signatures":"The 1812 Federal front in white marble with its arcade, colonnade and cupola, the circular rotunda with a coffered dome on ten columns, the self supporting curved twin stairs, the second floor gallery, the Governor's Room and its portrait collection, the park and its fountain.","daylit":true,"index":127,"place":"New York City Hall Rotunda","fact":"New York City Hall was completed in 1812 to designs by Joseph Francois Mangin and John McComb Jr. It is one of the oldest continuously used city halls in the nation and still houses its original government functions. The Landmarks Preservation Commission designated the building an individual landmark in 1966 and its central rotunda an interior landmark in 1976. Free tours pass through the rotunda and the Governor's Room.","year":"1812","learn":"The 1812 rotunda where New York still governs itself.","source":{"name":"NYC Public Design Commission","url":"https://www.nyc.gov/site/designcommission/archive/city-hall/about-city-hall.page"},"keywords":["New York City Hall rotunda","City Hall 1812","Mangin and McComb","City Hall interior landmark","Governor's Room City Hall","City Hall Park Manhattan"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"castleclinton","name":"Arrivals in the round","area":"CASTLE CLINTON / THE BATTERY","mood":"Harbor morning","color":"#be9775","description":"The Battery promenade, the harbor with the Statue on the horizon, gulls, a ferry passing, then the round brownstone fort: an open court under a flag, cannon at the embrasures, the ticket booth, the works set into the gun ports around the ring.","signatures":"The circular 1811 sandstone battery with its deep embrasures and arched gate, the open courtyard, the flagpole, the Battery lawn and benches, the seawall promenade, Liberty and the harbor beyond.","daylit":true,"index":128,"place":"Castle Clinton National Monument","fact":"Castle Clinton began as the Southwest Battery, built between 1808 and 1811 on rocks off the tip of Manhattan with 28 cannons. Renamed for DeWitt Clinton in 1817, it became the Castle Garden entertainment hall, where Jenny Lind made her American debut in 1850. From August 3, 1855 to April 18, 1890 it served as the nation's first immigrant landing depot, and more than 8 million people entered through it. It housed the New York Aquarium from 1896 to 1941 and reopened as a national monument in 1975.","year":"1855","learn":"A harbor fort that welcomed 8 million immigrants before Ellis Island.","source":{"name":"National Park Service","url":"https://www.nps.gov/cacl/learn/historyculture/index.htm"},"keywords":["Castle Clinton","Castle Garden immigration","the Battery Manhattan","Southwest Battery 1811","New York Aquarium Battery Park","Castle Clinton National Monument"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"greenacre","name":"The waterfall room","area":"GREENACRE PARK / EAST 51ST STREET","mood":"Lunch hour under the water","color":"#94b8a0","description":"East 51st Street, then the vest pocket park between two buildings: honey locusts, movable chairs, the raised west terrace under its trellis and acrylic domes, the runnel under the rough east wall, and the 25 foot waterfall filling the back, the works hung on the walls.","signatures":"The 1971 park by Sasaki, Dawson, DeMay, roughly sixty feet by one hundred twenty, a higher terrace along the west side under trellising, acrylic domes and heaters, honey locusts, a runnel along the ashlar east wall, and the 25 foot cascade across the back.","daylit":true,"index":129,"place":"Greenacre Park","fact":"Greenacre Park sits at 217 East 51st Street between Second and Third Avenues. Abby Rockefeller Mauze, inspired by Paley Park and the shortage of green space in Midtown, set up the Greenacre Foundation in 1968 to build it. The park officially opened on October 14, 1971. It was designed as an intimate room where the surrounding buildings act as walls and a canopy of leaves as the ceiling, with a waterfall at the back.","year":"1971","learn":"A Rockefeller built a waterfall room on East 51st Street in 1971.","source":{"name":"Greenacre Park","url":"https://www.greenacrepark.org/history"},"keywords":["Greenacre Park","217 East 51st Street","Abby Rockefeller Mauze","Midtown pocket park waterfall","Greenacre Foundation 1968","vest pocket park New York"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"paley","name":"Seventeen honey locusts","area":"PALEY PARK / EAST 53RD STREET","mood":"Dappled noon","color":"#acc4a1","description":"Granite steps up from 53rd Street into the original vest pocket park: seventeen honey locusts on a grid, vertical lawns of ivy, Bertoia wire chairs and Saarinen tables, and the water wall drowning the traffic, a portrait in every tree bay.","signatures":"The 1967 park by Robert Zion of Zion and Breen, a tenth of an acre on the site of the Stork Club, seventeen honey locusts on a grid, English ivy on both side walls, a twenty foot water wall across the back, 20 Saarinen tables and 60 Bertoia chairs, a kiosk at the entrance.","daylit":true,"index":130,"place":"Paley Park","fact":"Paley Park opened in 1967 on East 53rd Street, the original vest pocket park. Robert Zion of Zion and Breen Associates designed it on the site of the former Stork Club. CBS chairman William Paley provided the site and dedicated the park to his father, Samuel Paley. It covers one tenth of an acre, with a 20 foot wall of water at the back and seventeen honey locust trees planted on a grid above movable wire chairs and marble tables.","year":"1967","learn":"The Stork Club became a tenth of an acre of falling water.","source":{"name":"The Cultural Landscape Foundation","url":"https://www.tclf.org/landscapes/paley-park"},"keywords":["Paley Park","East 53rd Street waterfall","vest pocket park","Robert Zion","Stork Club site","William Paley"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"stmarksyard","name":"Poetry in the yard","area":"ST. MARK'S CHURCH IN-THE-BOWERY","mood":"East Village, a reading at eight","color":"#bdc5bb","description":"Second Avenue at Tenth Street: the fieldstone church of 1799 under its 1828 steeple, the cast iron portico, the lions, the flat gravestones of the old Stuyvesant farm, then the plain white sanctuary set with folding chairs for the Poetry Project.","signatures":"The fieldstone body of 1799, the 1828 steeple by Thompson and Town, the cast iron portico of 1856, Borglum's Aspiration and Inspiration, the lions of St. Mark, the flat vault markers, the Stuyvesant vault under the east wall and the Dupuis bust, the Edelman windows, the white sanctuary with folding chairs and a lectern.","daylit":true,"index":131,"place":"St. Mark's Church in-the-Bowery","fact":"Peter Stuyvesant built a chapel on his bouwerij farm in 1660 and was buried beneath it. The present fieldstone church at 131 East 10th Street, designed by John McComb Jr., was consecrated on May 9, 1799, and its steeple was added in 1828. The Poetry Project was founded here in 1966 and Danspace Project has been housed here since 1974. A three alarm fire on July 12, 1978 destroyed nine of 23 stained glass windows and part of the roof, and rebuilding took five years.","year":"1966","learn":"Stuyvesant's farm chapel became the East Village home of poetry and dance.","source":{"name":"St. Mark's Church in-the-Bowery","url":"https://stmarksbowery.org/history"},"keywords":["St. Mark's Church in-the-Bowery","Poetry Project 1966","Peter Stuyvesant grave","131 East 10th Street","East Village church 1799","Danspace Project"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"kingsfoyer","name":"Before the curtain","area":"KINGS THEATRE / FLATBUSH AVENUE","mood":"The grand foyer, doors at seven","color":"#c2a272","description":"Flatbush Avenue under the marquee and the vertical sign, through the bronze doors and the low ticket lobby into the foyer: gilt plaster, pink marble, a coffered ceiling with chandeliers, the red carpet to the grand stair, the auditorium curtain glimpsed through an arch.","signatures":"The 1929 Loew's Wonder Theatre by Rapp and Rapp, French Baroque after Versailles and the Paris Opera: the marquee and blade sign on Flatbush, the three storey foyer with gilt pilasters, pink marble columns, coffered ceilings, chandeliers, and the grand stair to the mezzanine.","daylit":false,"index":132,"place":"Kings Theatre","fact":"The Loew's Kings Theatre opened in Flatbush in 1929 as a movie and live performance house at 1027 Flatbush Avenue in Brooklyn. It struggled as multiplexes drew audiences away and shut its doors in 1977. The building sat dark for decades. After a 95 million dollar restoration the 3,074 seat theater reopened in 2015 in its original glory and now hosts concerts and live performances.","year":"2015","learn":"Flatbush's 1929 movie palace sat dark for decades and reopened in 2015.","source":{"name":"Time Out New York","url":"https://www.timeout.com/newyork/music/kings-theatre"},"keywords":["Kings Theatre Brooklyn","Loew's Kings Theatre","1027 Flatbush Avenue","Kings Theatre restoration 2015","Flatbush movie palace","Loew's Wonder Theatre"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"valencia","name":"A sky inside Jamaica","area":"LOEW'S VALENCIA / JAMAICA, QUEENS","mood":"1929, the clouds are on","color":"#b8a089","description":"John Eberson's atmospheric theatre: the auditorium is a Spanish courtyard at night, tiled roofs and balconies along the walls, a proscenium arch and a red curtain at the end, and over it all a deep blue sky with stars and drifting clouds. The works hang on the courtyard walls.","signatures":"The 1929 atmospheric auditorium: Spanish and Moorish courtyard facades with balconies, tiled roofs and corner towers, the proscenium arch, the blue plaster sky with its star bulbs and drifting clouds, the Wonder organ, and outside on Jamaica Avenue the Churrigueresque front of yellow brick and glazed terra cotta with its ticket booth and marquee. A 1929 inspired exhibition, not the present congregation's interior.","daylit":false,"index":133,"place":"Loew's Valencia Theatre","fact":"The Loew's Valencia at 165-11 Jamaica Avenue in Jamaica, Queens, was built in 1928 and opened in 1929 as the first of the five Loew's Wonder Theatres. Architect John Eberson, inventor of the atmospheric theater, gave it 3,554 seats and a brick and glazed terra cotta facade in the Spanish Baroque manner. It entertained Queens for half a century. Since 1977 it has housed the Tabernacle of Prayer for All People, and the city designated it a landmark on May 25, 1999.","year":"1929","learn":"The first Loew's Wonder Theatre now holds a church in Jamaica, Queens.","source":{"name":"NYC Landmarks Preservation Commission, LP-2036","url":"https://s-media.nyc.gov/agencies/lpc/lp/2036.pdf"},"keywords":["Loew's Valencia Theatre","Tabernacle of Prayer for All People","165-11 Jamaica Avenue","John Eberson atmospheric theater","Loew's Wonder Theatres","Jamaica Queens movie palace"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"essexmarket","name":"The city at the counter","area":"ESSEX MARKET / ESSEX CROSSING","mood":"Saturday at noon","color":"#b8c7b8","description":"Essex Street at Delancey, then the glass hall under its folded white ceiling: vendor islands under swaying pendants, fish on ice, the butcher, the coffee bar and its chalkboard, an escalator up to the long shared table on the mezzanine, the works on every stall front.","signatures":"The 2019 hall at Essex Crossing: a tall glazed street edge with the tower above, a folded plate ceiling with light in the folds, terrazzo, vendor islands and pendants, a moving escalator to the mezzanine, the communal table, the Delancey Street subway stair on the corner.","daylit":true,"index":134,"place":"Essex Market","fact":"Mayor Fiorello La Guardia built indoor municipal markets to clear the pushcart peddlers who had crowded Lower East Side streets since the 1880s. The Essex Street Municipal Market opened on January 9, 1940 with four buildings and 475 vendors serving Jewish and Italian immigrant families. After a 1995 consolidation the market operated at 120 Essex Street. In May 2019 the vendors moved across the street into a larger, modern hall at 88 Essex Street, part of the Essex Crossing development.","year":"1940","learn":"La Guardia moved the pushcarts indoors in 1940 and the market never left.","source":{"name":"Essex Market, Our Story","url":"https://www.essexmarket.nyc/our-story"},"keywords":["Essex Market","Essex Street Market 1940","Essex Crossing","88 Essex Street","La Guardia pushcart markets","Lower East Side market history"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"armstronggarden","name":"The garden listens","area":"LOUIS ARMSTRONG HOUSE / CORONA","mood":"A Queens afternoon","color":"#bc9c79","description":"107th Street in Corona: the brick house with its stoop, then the garden behind the fence, the trellis, the koi pond, the brick barbecue, a trumpet on a plinth and a record turning on the patio, the neighbours passing, the 7 train on the elevated a few blocks off, the works hung on the fence, the trellis and the house wall.","signatures":"The two storey brick house Louis and Lucille bought in 1943 on a block of houses like it, the stoop, the side garden Lucille planted with its trellis, koi pond and brick barbecue, wind chimes, utility poles, the elevated 7 train on the horizon.","daylit":true,"index":135,"place":"Louis Armstrong House Museum","fact":"Louis and Lucille Armstrong moved into their home on 107th Street in Corona, Queens in 1943 and lived there for nearly 30 years, until Louis's death in 1971. The house is both a National Historic Landmark and a New York City landmark. Its archives opened to researchers in the 1990s and the house opened for guided tours in 2003. Across the street, the 14,000 square foot Louis Armstrong Center opened in 2023 with a 75 seat venue and a garden.","year":"1943","learn":"Satchmo lived in a modest Corona house from 1943 until he died.","source":{"name":"Louis Armstrong House Museum","url":"https://www.louisarmstronghouse.org/about/"},"keywords":["Louis Armstrong House Museum","Louis Armstrong Corona Queens","34-56 107th Street","Louis Armstrong Center 2023","Lucille Armstrong","Corona Queens landmark"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"hamiltongrange","name":"A house above the city","area":"HAMILTON GRANGE / ST. NICHOLAS PARK","mood":"Porch light","color":"#c2cab7","description":"St. Nicholas Avenue, the park wall, then the climb: a gravel path switchbacking up the hill past the schist outcrop and the thirteen sweetgums to the pale yellow Federal house with its green shutters and wide piazza. Inside, the hall of miniatures and the two octagonal parlors, the piano, the dining table, a hearth lit for the afternoon, the works in gilt on the papered walls.","signatures":"The 1802 country house moved into St. Nicholas Park in 2008: clapboard painted pale yellow, green shutters, a hipped roof with a balustrade, piazzas on two sides, the twin octagonal rooms with their mirrored doors, the thirteen sweetgum trees, the Manhattan schist outcrop, City College on the ridge behind.","daylit":true,"index":136,"place":"Hamilton Grange National Memorial","fact":"Alexander Hamilton commissioned architect John McComb Jr. to build the Grange, his Federal style country house in upper Manhattan, in 1802. He lived there only two years before the 1804 duel with Aaron Burr ended his life. As the city grew around it, the house was moved twice, in 1889 and again in 2008, to keep it standing. It now sits in St. Nicholas Park at 414 West 141st Street and reopened to the public in 2011 as a National Park Service memorial.","year":"1802","learn":"Hamilton's country house was rolled twice to survive the city around it.","source":{"name":"National Park Service","url":"https://www.nps.gov/hagr/index.htm"},"keywords":["Hamilton Grange","Alexander Hamilton house Harlem","St. Nicholas Park","414 West 141st Street","Hamilton Grange move 2008","John McComb Jr."],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"fultonfish","name":"Four in the morning","area":"THE NEW FULTON FISH MARKET / HUNTS POINT","mood":"Cold store, before dawn","color":"#7fb2c8","description":"The hour the city eats by and never sees: a refrigerated hall the length of three blocks, ice going down in mountains, forklifts running the centre aisle with their beacons turning, gulls working the rafters, and the buyers walking the row in headlamps and parkas. The New Yorkers hang on the stall fronts, at the height of a chalked price.","signatures":"The 400,000 square foot cold hall at Hunts Point, the numbered bays and their roll up doors, stainless display tables banked with crushed ice, waxed boxes stacked to the ceiling, hanging scales, chalkboards, forklift beacons, the truck apron outside, and the gulls that came with the market from South Street.","daylit":false,"index":137,"place":"The New Fulton Fish Market","fact":"The Fulton Fish Market began in 1822 as one wing of the Fulton Market on the East River waterfront. On November 14, 2005 it left the South Street Seaport after 183 years for a climate controlled hall at Hunts Point in the Bronx, built for about 85 million dollars and covering roughly 400,000 square feet. It is the largest fish market in the United States and second in the world only to Tokyo, and it works through the night.","year":"2005","learn":"A 183 year old market moved to the Bronx and kept its hours.","source":{"name":"Fulton Fish Market, Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fulton_Fish_Market"},"keywords":["Fulton Fish Market","Hunts Point fish market","New Fulton Fish Market","South Street Seaport fish market","wholesale seafood New York"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"lunarnewyear","name":"The lion comes down Mott Street","area":"CHINATOWN / MOTT STREET","mood":"Confetti, drums, first day","color":"#d8342c","description":"The narrowest parade in New York: a lion at every doorway, a dragon the length of half a block, confetti coming down like weather and not stopping, drums off the brick, lanterns strung overhead, and the whole street shoulder to shoulder in red. The New Yorkers hang on the shutters and the gates, at eye level, where the crowd is.","signatures":"The Mott Street canyon of five storey tenements with fire escapes, bilingual vertical signs, roll down shutters, red lanterns strung across the roadway, lion dance teams working doorway to doorway, a dragon on poles, drums and cymbals, and confetti ankle deep by afternoon.","daylit":false,"index":138,"place":"Chinatown, Mott Street","fact":"Better Chinatown USA held the first Chinatown Lunar New Year Parade and Festival in 1996, and added the New Year Firecracker Ceremony in 1998 at Mott and Bayard Streets. The crowds grew so large that the ceremony moved, first to Chatham Square and then to Sara D. Roosevelt Park. The parade still forms on Mott Street at Hester and works east and north through the oldest streets of the neighbourhood.","year":"1996","learn":"The parade is younger than the neighbourhood that invented it.","source":{"name":"Better Chinatown USA","url":"https://betterchinatown.com/lny-parade/"},"keywords":["Chinatown Lunar New Year Parade","firecracker ceremony Chinatown","Mott Street parade","lion dance New York","Lunar New Year NYC"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"enginecompany","name":"First due","area":"ENGINE COMPANY 55 / 363 BROOME STREET","mood":"The bell, then the doors","color":"#a8231f","description":"A house that has been answering this corner since 1899: one bay, one rig, a brass pole through the ceiling, gear hanging ready in the order a person puts it on. Every twenty seconds the gong goes, the beacons start turning, and the door rolls up on Broome Street. The New Yorkers hang along the tiled bay walls, watching it happen.","signatures":"R. H. Robertson's 1899 firehouse: brick and limestone with the company banner carved over a monumental arched apparatus bay, a copper mansard roof, oval windows in garlands, Guastavino glazed tile inside, the brass pole, turnout gear on hooks, the housewatch desk and the gong.","daylit":true,"index":139,"place":"Engine Company 55, 363 Broome Street","fact":"Engine Company 55 was organized on June 4, 1887 and moved into this purpose built house in March 1899. R. H. Robertson designed it in 1898, his only commission for the Fire Department, in brick and Indiana limestone with the company banner carved over the arched apparatus bay, a copper mansard roof and glazed Guastavino tile inside that could be hosed down after a run.","year":"1899","learn":"A church architect built one firehouse, and it is still in service.","source":{"name":"Landmarks Preservation Commission, Fire Engine Company 55","url":"https://s-media.nyc.gov/agencies/lpc/lp/1987.pdf"},"keywords":["Engine Company 55","363 Broome Street firehouse","R. H. Robertson firehouse","FDNY landmark firehouse","Guastavino tile firehouse"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"eastriverfourth","name":"The Fourth over the East River","area":"BROOKLYN HEIGHTS PROMENADE","mood":"Nine forty, everybody quiet","color":"#f0c24a","description":"A third of a mile of cantilevered walkway over a running expressway, and once a year the best free seat in the city: barges on the black water, the skyline with its lights on, and eighty thousand shells going up in half an hour. The New Yorkers hang along the park wall and the railing, lit red, then green, then gold, then not at all.","signatures":"The Promenade on its cantilever over the Brooklyn Queens Expressway, the iron railing and the benches, the plane trees, the Brooklyn Bridge to the north, fireboats and barges on the river, the Lower Manhattan skyline across the water, and the crowd standing four deep at the rail.","daylit":false,"index":140,"place":"The Brooklyn Heights Promenade","fact":"Macy's first large fireworks show went up on July 1, 1958 for the store's hundredth anniversary, and became a Fourth of July tradition in 1976 for the Bicentennial. Recent shows fire on the order of 80,000 shells over about half an hour from barges in the East River and from the Brooklyn Bridge itself. The Promenade that watches it is a roof: a public walkway cantilevered over two stacked decks of expressway, opened in 1950 and 1951.","year":"1958","learn":"The best free seat in the city is the lid on an expressway.","source":{"name":"Macy's 4th of July Fireworks, Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macy%27s_4th_of_July_Fireworks"},"keywords":["Macy's fireworks East River","Brooklyn Heights Promenade","Fourth of July New York","fireworks Brooklyn Bridge","BQE cantilever promenade"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"paradisegarage","name":"Two in the morning","area":"THE PARADISE GARAGE / 84 KING STREET","mood":"Members only, no bar","color":"#b83aa8","description":"A parking garage on King Street with a ramp for a lobby, a sprung floor, a sound system built for the room rather than the room for the system, and no liquor licence because nobody needed one. The New Yorkers hang where the light sweeps past them, on the black walls around the floor, and the mirror ball puts the rest of the room on top of them.","signatures":"The 1924 garage at 84 King Street, the long entrance ramp, the sprung wooden dance floor, Richard Long's custom horns and bass bins, the DJ booth above the floor, a mirror ball, no bar and no alcohol, the fruit and juice by the wall, and the doors that stayed open into the morning.","daylit":false,"index":141,"place":"The Paradise Garage, 84 King Street","fact":"Michael Brody leased a disused parking garage at 84 King Street in 1977 and, after a year of building, opened the Paradise Garage properly on January 28, 1978. Larry Levan was the resident for the life of the club. Richard Long built the sound system to the shape of the room, over a sprung floor holding about fourteen hundred people. It was members only and served no alcohol, so there was no last call. It closed in the autumn of 1987 and the building came down in April 2018.","year":"1978","learn":"No bar, no last call, and the name of a whole genre.","source":{"name":"Paradise Garage, Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Garage"},"keywords":["Paradise Garage","Larry Levan","84 King Street","garage house music","Richard Long sound system"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"arthurashe","name":"The night session","area":"ARTHUR ASHE STADIUM / FLUSHING MEADOWS","mood":"Deuce, under the open roof","color":"#2f6fd6","description":"The largest tennis stadium in the world at nine at night in the first week of September: the roof open to the sky, twenty three thousand people in the bowl, a plane on its way down to LaGuardia, and two players trading ground strokes on the blue court while the wave goes round. The New Yorkers hang courtside on the wall at the foot of the lower bowl, where the sponsor boards would be, lit by the same lights as the match.","signatures":"The 1997 bowl by Rossetti with its 23,771 seats, the 2016 retractable roof and its octagonal truss ring, the electric blue court inside the lines and green outside, the net and the chair umpire, line judges and ball kids, the two big screens, the light ring under the roof, and the approach path to LaGuardia overhead.","daylit":false,"index":142,"place":"Arthur Ashe Stadium","fact":"Arthur Ashe Stadium opened on August 25, 1997 at the USTA National Tennis Center in Flushing Meadows, Queens, designed by Rossetti at a cost of about 254 million dollars. With 23,771 seats it is the largest tennis stadium in the world. A retractable roof of two 800 ton fabric panels was completed in 2016 as part of a 550 million dollar renovation. It is named for Arthur Ashe, who won the first US Open in 1968, the first in which professionals could compete.","year":"1997","learn":"The biggest tennis stadium on earth is in Queens, and it got a roof in 2016.","source":{"name":"Arthur Ashe Stadium, Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arthur_Ashe_Stadium"},"keywords":["Arthur Ashe Stadium","US Open night session","largest tennis stadium","Flushing Meadows tennis","USTA Billie Jean King National Tennis Center"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"carousel","name":"Fifty seven horses","area":"THE CENTRAL PARK CAROUSEL / MID PARK AT 64TH","mood":"Three quarter time, all afternoon","color":"#d84a3a","description":"A 1908 carousel found in a Coney Island trolley terminal and set down in Central Park in 1951 under an octagon of brick: fifty seven hand carved horses, two chariots, a band organ playing waltzes, and a quarter of a million riders a year. The New Yorkers hang on the six brick walls around the turning platform and outside on the two that face the path, so the horses pass every one of them once a minute.","signatures":"The brick pavilion with its eight sides, arched entrances and pyramid roof, the turning platform with three rows of jumpers and standers, the poles, the sweeps, the rounding board of bulbs and mirrors, the centre drum with the A. Ruth & Sohn band organ, the ticket booth, and the park path and plane trees around it.","daylit":true,"index":143,"place":"The Central Park Carousel","fact":"The Central Park Carousel was made by Solomon Stein and Harry Goldstein in 1908 and carries 57 hand carved horses, 52 jumpers and 5 standers, and two chariots. It first ran in a trolley terminal in Coney Island until the 1940s and was moved to Central Park in 1951, the fourth carousel on the site since 1871, when the first was powered by a mule or horse under the platform. Its predecessors burned in 1924 and 1950. A 52 keyless A. Ruth & Sohn Model 33 band organ plays waltzes, marches and polkas, and about 250,000 people ride it every year.","year":"1908","learn":"Fifty seven horses carved in 1908, found in a Coney Island trolley barn and set down in the park in 1951.","source":{"name":"Central Park Carousel, Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Park_Carousel"},"keywords":["Central Park Carousel","Friedsam Memorial Carousel","Stein and Goldstein carousel","Central Park rides","Coney Island trolley terminal carousel"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"sharks","name":"Ocean Wonders","area":"NEW YORK AQUARIUM / CONEY ISLAND","mood":"Blue, and something circling","color":"#1f7fc8","description":"The oldest aquarium in the country, on the boardwalk since 1957, and its biggest room since the hurricane: seven hundred and eighty four thousand gallons behind one long window, a wreck on the sand, sand tigers circling, rays flying over the glass tunnel, and a school of silver that turns as one. The New Yorkers hang on the dark wall opposite the window, lit by the water.","signatures":"The long acrylic window into Ocean Wonders: Sharks!, the walk through tunnel, the shipwreck and the reef, sand tiger sharks, cownose rays and a bait ball, light rays and caustics on the sand, bubbles from the aerators, and the dark hall with its benches facing the water.","daylit":false,"index":144,"place":"The New York Aquarium","fact":"The New York Aquarium opened in 1896 at Castle Garden in Battery Park and moved to the boardwalk at Coney Island on June 6, 1957; it is the oldest continually operating aquarium in the United States and is run by the Wildlife Conservation Society. Hurricane Sandy flooded it in October 2012, with staff on site saving about 80 percent of the animals. Ocean Wonders: Sharks!, a 784,000 gallon, 57,000 square foot exhibit, broke ground in January 2014 and opened on June 30, 2018, the first major exhibit to open after the storm. The aquarium keeps 266 species on 14 acres.","year":"2018","learn":"The oldest aquarium in the country started in a fort at the Battery and now keeps sharks on the boardwalk.","source":{"name":"New York Aquarium, Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Aquarium"},"keywords":["New York Aquarium","Ocean Wonders Sharks","Coney Island aquarium","oldest aquarium in the United States","Wildlife Conservation Society aquarium"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"astoriapool","name":"The finest in the world","area":"ASTORIA PARK POOL / QUEENS","mood":"Ninety three degrees, everybody in","color":"#3ab6cc","description":"Three hundred and thirty feet of water between two bridges, opened in the summer of 1936 and called the finest in the world by the man who paid for it. Lap swimmers, cannonballs, lifeguards on their chairs, the diving tower that outlived its pool, and Hell Gate to the north. The New Yorkers hang under the colonnade of the bathhouse, out of the sun, facing the water.","signatures":"The WPA pool of 1936 at 330 by 165 feet, the brick and limestone bathhouse with its central block and colonnade, lane ropes and ladders, lifeguard chairs, the filled in diving pool and its 32 foot three tier concrete platform, the Hell Gate Bridge arch to the north and the RFK Bridge towers to the south, the East River and Manhattan beyond.","daylit":true,"index":145,"place":"Astoria Park Pool","fact":"Astoria Park Pool opened on July 2, 1936, the largest of the eleven pools the Works Progress Administration completed in New York City that summer; WPA administrator Harry Hopkins called it the finest in the world. The main pool is 330 by 165 feet and four feet deep, 54,450 square feet of water, and at its peak the pool area held 6,200 swimmers. It hosted the United States Olympic trials in 1936, 1952 and 1964. The diving pool, with its 32 foot three tier cantilevered platform, was drained in the 1980s and filled in between 2017 and 2019; the platform still stands. The complex was designated a city landmark in 2006.","year":"1936","learn":"The city's biggest pool opened in 1936 between two bridges and held the Olympic trials three times.","source":{"name":"Astoria Park, Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astoria_Park"},"keywords":["Astoria Park Pool","WPA pools 1936","largest pool in New York City","Astoria Pool Olympic trials","Astoria Park diving platform"],"article":null,"articles":[]},{"id":"rockcenter","name":"The tree and the rink","area":"ROCKEFELLER PLAZA / THE RINK","mood":"December, seven in the evening","color":"#d8a84a","description":"The sunken rink at the foot of the Channel Gardens, Prometheus in gold over the fountain, forty flags going round the parapet, skaters below and a Norway spruce above them with fifty thousand lights on it. The New Yorkers hang along the shop fronts of the Channel Gardens, between the wire angels, and down on the rink level either side of the fountain.","signatures":"The Channel Gardens sloping down from Fifth Avenue between the British Empire Building and La Maison Francaise, the trumpeting wire angels, the sunken rink with its flags and skaters, Paul Manship's gilded Prometheus and the fountain, the Christmas tree with its star, and 30 Rockefeller Plaza rising behind it.","daylit":false,"index":146,"place":"Rockefeller Center, the tree and the rink","fact":"The first tree at Rockefeller Center was a 20 foot balsam fir put up on Christmas Eve 1931 by the men building the complex, decorated with strings of cranberries, garlands of paper and a few tin cans. The first official tree, 50 feet tall, was lit in 1933, and the skating rink opened in the sunken plaza below it in 1936. The tree is usually a Norway spruce of 69 to 100 feet carrying about 50,000 LED lights; the tallest, in 1999, was a 100 foot spruce from Killingworth, Connecticut. The star placed in 2018 has 70 spikes and three million crystals and weighs 900 pounds. The tree is lit on the Wednesday after Thanksgiving, and since 2007 each one has been milled into lumber for Habitat for Humanity.","year":"1931","learn":"The tree began with construction workers and cranberries in 1931; the rink below it opened in 1936.","source":{"name":"Rockefeller Center Christmas Tree, Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rockefeller_Center_Christmas_Tree"},"keywords":["Rockefeller Center Christmas Tree","Rockefeller Center rink","Rockefeller Center tree lighting","Prometheus Rockefeller Center","Channel Gardens angels"],"article":null,"articles":[]}];
